var SEARCH=[{"t":"Progression","u":"guide-progression.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nMine bauxite ore and smelt it into aluminum. Blast iron into steel, then craft copper wire, silicon components, and server modules. Place a server rack, fill its eight bays  one module per bay, any size , and connect its power face through a PDU and power cable to a generator or solar source. Diesel generators accept vanilla furnace fuel and the Rackcraft fuels below. See  Cooling  for keeping racks below 27 C.\n\nThe machine screens open by right-clicking. Rack bays accept  pi_node ,  server_1u ,  asic_miner ,  gpu_blade ,  tensor_accelerator ,  quantum_core , the crafting modules and  wafer_scale_engine , one per bay; a quantum core only contributes load when a CDU is adjacent. Power, coolant, and fiber cables form independent networks.\n\n"},{"t":"Multiplayer: Companies","u":"guide-multiplayer-companies.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOn a server, players run   companies  . Anyone who hasn't founded or joined one runs the world's  default company , which owns everything built before companies existed. Singleplayer worlds and old saves work exactly as before.\n\n-   Commands:    /company create <name> ,  invite <player> ,  join <name> ,  leave ,  kick <player>   leader ,  rename <name>   leader ,  color <colour>   leader ,  pay <amount> <name> ,  list  and  info  name  .\n-   What's separate:   each company has its own RackCoin balance, research, AI business, orbit, warheads, orbital strikes, darknet auctions and HEROBRINE-1, and gets its own advancements and news.\n-   One balance in every dimension:   a company's balance, research, orbit, Exchange prices, AI business and darknet are the same in the Overworld, the Nether, the End and any other dimension, so racks anywhere pay into one balance. Older saves kept a separate, hidden balance and orbit per dimension; on load they're added to the main ones.\n-   Ownership:   a machine works for the company of whoever built it, whether a player placed it or a Site Planner's drones, a skid or a rocket made it. It mines into that company's balance and runs on its research.\n-   Rivals are locked out:   they can't open or configure your machines, see your drives  even on shared fiber , link a Wireless Terminal or Uplink to your transmitter, or ride your teleport pads.\n-   Sabotage is allowed:   the world is shared, so rivals can still break blocks, cut cables, smog you o"},{"t":"Other Mods: JEI, Jade, Create and Energy","u":"guide-other-mods-jei-jade-create-and-energy.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nAll optional: Rackcraft plays the same without them.\n\n-   Grid Interface  any energy mod :   swaps Rackcraft power with Team Reborn Energy, the \"E\" most Fabric tech mods use  bundled, so the block always works . Energy pushed into it becomes power on the network it touches, at  energy.importEPerKw   3  E/t for each kW. With Crafts & Additions, an Alternator on a Create shaft at full stress  180 E/t from 16,384 SU  gives 60 kW, about 1 kW per 256 SU: a real first power source that doesn't beat diesel. Once   Grid Export Permit   is researched  10,000 RC and 500k compute, no prerequisites , it also sends power out into anything beside it that takes energy, such as an Electric Motor. That direction is deliberately poor value, because the grid scales to gigawatts:  energy.exportEPerKw   0.25, so 4 kW for each E/t, about 15 kW per 256 SU through a motor , at most  energy.maxExportPerBlock   240 E/t, one motor at full stress  a block and  energy.maxExportPerCompany   1,200 E/t  across a company's interfaces. Each direction holds  energy.buffer   5,000 E . An interface with energy waiting to come in doesn't send any out, so it never feeds itself. Create-focused packs can rebalance all of it in the config.\n-   Create recipes:   with Create installed, Crushing Wheels crush Bauxite Ore  1 Raw Bauxite, 50% a second  and Failed Modules  2 Silicon, 50% a Copper Wire , a Mechanical Press makes 4 Copper Wire from an ingot  a crafting table makes 6 , and a heated Basin mixes an iron ingot "},{"t":"Field Manual","u":"guide-field-manual.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nCraft a book with a copper ingot to get the Rackcraft Field Manual, then right-click to open it. It has a chapter for each stage of progression and a page for every block and item, and it shows the recipes the game has actually loaded, so datapack changes appear automatically. Click a Rackcraft ingredient to jump to its page. Use the arrow keys or the scroll wheel to turn pages, and Backspace to go back. Long pages continue onto the next page, so no recipe is cut off.\n\n"},{"t":"Mining RackCoin and the Crypto Exchange","u":"guide-mining-rackcoin-and-the-crypto-exchange.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nRacks mine RackCoin  RC  into one balance shared by the whole world. A rack mines when it has modules, at least 50% power, a fiber link to a router, and an intake below 40 C. Racks   boot   once powered: 2 seconds per Pi Node, 4 per 1U Server, 5 per ASIC, 8 per GPU Blade or Tensor Accelerator and 15 per Quantum Core, so a full rack of Quantum Cores takes two minutes. Mining, power draw and heat ramp up as they boot, and a rack that loses power starts from cold again.  sim.rackBootScale  in the config scales boot times. Open a rack to see its live RC/s, or the reason it has stopped and how to fix it. Spend RackCoin at a   Crypto Exchange  : its recipe is glass panes, two CPU chips, a circuit board, steel and an emerald. Its curated tabs sell bundles of resources, rare items and Rackcraft parts   ExchangeOffers.java  . The   All Items   tab sells almost every survival item, searchable, priced from base values plus recipe costs   ExchangeCatalog.java  ; creative-only items are excluded. Click buys one, Shift-click ten, Ctrl-click a stack. Prices work like an exchange of value for value: raw materials have a base price, everything made from them costs what went in plus 10%, and then   everything except plain building blocks costs double  . Materials, ores, storage blocks, tools, parts and machines are what RackCoin is for, while stone bricks and stairs stay cheap. Rackcraft's own hardware costs more again, so a big balance can't simply buy a whole facility: components  chips, b"},{"t":"Compute: Mining, Autocrafting and AI Work","u":"guide-compute-mining-autocrafting-and-ai-work.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nModules stack to four in your inventory  Failed Modules to sixteen , but every rack bay holds exactly one: shift-clicking a stack into a rack fills that many empty bays, and hoppers only fill empty bays.\n\nRacks on one fiber network form a   cluster  . Each step the facility hands out whole racks, in this order: autocrafting jobs, then AI contracts that are generating  earliest deadline first , then model training. Racks nothing needs keep mining. A rack lent out doesn't mine, but it draws the same power.\n\n-   Autocrafting   borrows racks from the storage's own fiber network first, then from any   online   cluster  one with an uplink or core router . Before, it only used racks wired straight to the storage, so a storage network with no racks of its own reported \"No compute\" forever.\n-   Cluster policy    set at the Operations Terminal :  Auto  lends racks to any work;  Mining only  never lends.\n- Every module lends   general compute   to autocrafting and   AI compute   to contracts and training:\n\n  Module   Mines  RC/s    General   AI   Cooling  \n  ---   ---   ---   ---   ---  \n  Pi Node   0.5   1   0.4   air  \n  1U Server   2   2   1.6   air  \n  ASIC Miner   5   0   0   liquid  \n  GPU Blade   12   6   10   liquid  \n  Tensor Accelerator   0   3   12   liquid  \n  Quantum Core   60   20   50   liquid  \n  Crafting Coprocessor   0   8   0.5   air  \n  Crafting Accelerator   0   30   2   liquid  \n  Wafer-Scale Engine   0   40   150   liquid  2 to 16 kW; only a Wafer Fab makes it  "},{"t":"Cables","u":"guide-cables.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nPower cables, coolant pipes, fiber and item pipes connect only toward cables of the same kind and toward machines on that network, forming straight runs, corners and junctions. Cables placed with older versions are upgraded when their chunk loads. See  Building and Wiring  for Trunk Bundles, Patch Panels, Pylons and the tools that lay cable for you.\n\n"},{"t":"Building and Wiring","u":"guide-building-and-wiring.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe tools for when the base outgrows the cable. Everything here is crafted from parts you already make  steel, circuit boards, Superconducting Wire, Hydrogen Canisters , and the machines wait for their research.\n\n-   Trunk Bundle:   one thick conduit carrying power, coolant and fiber at once, each on its own lane. It joins any cable of those kinds it touches, and machines on those networks, so no tap blocks are needed: run a trunk down the hall and branch off with ordinary cable. A cut trunk cuts all three lanes, and one Repair Kit splice mends them. The Multimeter reads each lane. Crafted four at a time from one power cable, one coolant pipe, one fiber cable, two steel ingots and an aluminum ingot. Inert until   Structured Cabling  .\n-   Cable Planner:   use a block face to set the start, another for the end, and a ghost of the route hangs in the world: cyan outlines for the cable, red where something is in the way, dim green where the cable already is. Use in the air to cycle the route style  Straight, Zig-zag, Shortest, Hug the walls , sneak-use in the air to cycle the cable  power, coolant, fiber, item pipe, trunk . A third use on any block lays the route from your inventory  or a Wireless Terminal's storage , and stops at the first thing in the way. Sneak-use on a block clears it. Routes are limited to 512 blocks.\n-   Cable paint:   use any dye on a cable to paint it, or sneak-use to paint the whole run  the connected cables of that kind with the same paint, up to 1,02"},{"t":"Fuels","u":"guide-fuels.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe diesel generator burns one item at a time. Each tick of furnace burn time gives one tick of 40 kW output. Fuel items show their runtime in their tooltip, and a lava bucket leaves its empty bucket in the generator's slot.\n\n  Fuel   Recipe   Runtime  \n  ---   ---   ---  \n  Biomass Pellet   8   rackcraft:biomass   crops, seeds, kelp, leaves, saplings  around bone meal, makes 4   40 s  \n  Coke   Blast coal or charcoal   160 s  \n  Biodiesel Canister   Aluminum ingot over 6 biomass pellets   480 s  \n  Block of Coke   9 coke   1,600 s  \n\n"},{"t":"Cooling","u":"guide-cooling.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nRack intake temperature controls thermal throttling: performance begins to fall above 27 C and trips at 40 C. Empty server racks are valid and can be configured before modules are installed.\n\nEvery kilowatt a rack draws comes back out as heat, and a rack slows down once the air at its front passes 27 C and stops at 40 C. An unpowered rack makes no heat. Heat leaves a rack two ways, and the rack screen shows both    To loop   and   To air   :\n\n-   Air.   Out of the back into the room. Air heat spreads block by block and leaks away: slowly through walls, quickly outdoors. Keep it off the intakes with hot and cold aisles, blanking panels and raised floor, or catch it:\n  -   CDU on the back  : a CDU placed against a rack's back face catches up to 40 kW of that rack's exhaust into its coolant loop, so the CDU a Quantum Core rack needs can sit behind it without trapping the exhaust. Any other solid block behind a rack pushes its exhaust out sideways, often into the next aisle.\n  -   CRAC unit  : pulls up to 40 kW per face out of the room air into its coolant loop  3 kW .\n  -   Exhaust Fan  : vents up to 10 kW from the block in front of it outside  0.2 kW . It only runs, and only makes smog, while that air is at least 1 C above ambient.\n-   Loop.   Each connected run of   Coolant Pipe   is one coolant loop. Liquid-cooled modules  ASIC and up  send 85% of their heat down the pipe, and a rack holding them stops with  Needs liquid cooling  until it is on a loop with at least one heat"},{"t":"Wind and Solar","u":"guide-wind-and-solar.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\n  Source   Output   Notes  \n  ---   ---   ---  \n    Solar Panel     4 kW   Daytime, open sky, dimmed by smog  \n    Wind Turbine     Up to 30 kW   25% at Y 70 or below, full at Y 130, +50% in thunderstorms; no fuel and no upkeep  \n    Solar Array     150 kW in full sun   A placed 3x2 built on the Assembly Line  6 Solar Panels, 2 Circuit Boards, weld, rivet . Arrays that touch share power with no cables  \n    Wind Tower     600 kW at Y 130, up to 750 kW above Y 150   A Wind Tower Nacelle  built on the line  on at least ten Tower Sections, with a clear 5x5 in front for its blades. Power runs down the tower  \n\nThe wind is one for the whole world: it rises and falls by up to a quarter over a few minutes, for every turbine and tower at once, so a battery bank evens out a wind farm. Solar Arrays and Wind Towers wear: over three days of running they lose up to 30% of their output, until a   Maintenance Drone   from a Drone Dock within 32 blocks services them  the dock sends one at 25% wear . The basic panel and turbine never wear, so an early setup never depends on drones. Every number here is in the  renewables  section of the config.\n\n"},{"t":"Utility Plants","u":"guide-utility-plants.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nTwo more cube multiblocks  2x2x2 to 5x5x5 , each doing something no other machine does. Their casings change when formed; they make no items, so they have no port.\n\n  Plant   What it does   Numbers  \n  ---   ---   ---  \n    Grid-Tie Substation     Sells spare solar, wind and reactor output to the outside grid for RackCoin, once everything else is powered and batteries are charged. Never drains a battery, runs a diesel or resells the utility feed; exported reactor power still burns fuel   Up to 1 MW per core; 0.05 RC per kJ at night, 60% of that before dusk, 2.5 times as much in the evening peak  \n    Heat Recovery Plant     A coolant-loop heat sink that sells the heat to nearby villages as district heating. Needs no power or water, only customers   50 kW per villager within 64 blocks  half again in cold biomes, less in hot ones , up to 400 kW per core; 0.4 RC per kJ  \n\n"},{"t":"Grid-Scale Batteries","u":"guide-grid-scale-batteries.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nA Battery Bank stores 3,000 kJ, charges at 15 kW and discharges at 60 kW, losing 10% each way. Build Battery Banks into a solid cube, 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5, and they become one   Grid-Scale Battery  : one store with every bank's charge and rates, 10% more capacity per bank for each step up in size  40% more in a 5-cube , and smaller losses: 8% each way for a 2-cube down to 2% for a 5-cube. The casing changes when it forms, and its screen and the Multimeter show its size, capacity and efficiency.\n\nTwo higher tiers are made on the Assembly Line, each forming its own Grid-Scale Battery cubes with the same size bonuses:\n\n  Bank   Store   Charge / discharge   Made from   Research  \n  ---   ---   ---   ---   ---  \n  Battery Bank   3,000 kJ   15 / 60 kW   Crafted   None  \n    Lithium-Ion Battery Bank     30,000 kJ   150 / 500 kW   Battery Bank + 8 Battery Cells + 2 Circuit Boards, weld, rivet   Grid Storage Chemistry  \n    Solid-State Battery Bank     250,000 kJ   1,200 / 4,000 kW   Lithium-Ion bank + 4 Graphene Sheets + 4 Gallium Nitride + 2 Superconducting Wire, weld, rivet   Solid-State Cells  \n\nA 5x5x5 of Lithium-Ion banks holds over 5 GJ: about a megawatt for an hour and a half.\n\nThe Diesel Generator has a big brother too: the   Gas Turbine    a Diesel Generator + 4 Electric Motors + 12 Steel + 2 Circuit Boards on the Assembly Line, after   Combined-Cycle Turbines    makes 400 kW from the same fuels, burning them eight times as fast, so each lump goes a quarter further. It spins "},{"t":"Nuclear","u":"guide-nuclear.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nEvery nuclear machine is a   cube multiblock  : build a solid cube of the same machine, 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5, and it works as one. Its casing changes to show it, and every kind of cube has its own face: a reactor's trefoil, a centrifuge's rotors, a fab's wafer. Every cube that makes items has a   port  : its bottom north-west corner, framed in cyan with an output hatch. Products, by-products and a reactor's Spent Fuel gather there, and the port's screen has a   Collect all   button that hands you everything the whole cube holds. A processing cube short of power runs at the share it gets  a cube with a third of its power works at a third of the speed  and only stops below 10%; its screen shows what it needs and how much of that the grid covers. Every block is a core, items put into any core are shared evenly across the cube, and bigger cubes are more economical: 5% less fuel or power per core for a 2-cube, up to 20% for a 5-cube. Anything that isn't a whole cube shows an amber \"not formed\" fault.\n\n  Step   Machine   In   Out   Per batch, per core  \n  ---   ---   ---   ---   ---  \n  Mine   Uranium Ore  Y -64 to 16, iron pickaxe      Raw Uranium    \n  Mill   Uranium Mill   2 Raw Uranium   Yellowcake   30 s, 20 kW  \n  Enrich   Gas Centrifuge   6 Yellowcake   Enriched Uranium + 4 Depleted Uranium   120 s, 60 kW  \n  Fabricate   Fuel Fabricator   Enriched Uranium + 2 Steel Ingots   Fuel Cell   60 s, 30 kW  \n  Burn   Modular Reactor   Fuel Cell   500 kW for 30 min, then Spent Fuel    "},{"t":"Storage and Autocrafting","u":"guide-storage-and-autocrafting.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\n-   Storage Array:   8 bays for 1K / 4K / 16K / 64K / 256K / 1M   Storage Drives  . The two biggest are upgrades on the Assembly Line, and a drive keeps everything stored on it through the upgrade: a 64K drive with 8 RAM Modules and 2 Circuit Boards  weld  becomes a   256K Storage Drive    262,144 items, after   Shingled Recording   ; a 256K drive with 2 Photonic Chips and 2 HBM Stacks  rivet  becomes a   1M Storage Drive    1,048,576 items, after   Holographic Storage   . It works like a rack: it needs power  0.4 kW + 0.15 kW per drive , takes air in the front and exhausts heat out the back, and goes offline  nothing is lost  when unpowered or at 40 C.\n-   Tape Library:   4 bays for   Tape Cartridges   of 1,048,576 items each. This is cold storage: 0.3 kW, but players wait 2 seconds for each read. When drives pass 85% full, the least recently used items are archived to tape automatically.\n-   Storage Terminal:   a searchable grid of everything on its fiber network, a 3x3 crafting grid that refills from storage, and pattern encoding. Left-click takes a stack, right-click half, Shift-click to inventory; clicking with a held item deposits it. Items only on tape are tinted blue.\n-   Autocrafting runs on your racks.   Encode a   Recipe Pattern   from a   Blank Pattern   and store it on the network; its output shows a  + . Middle-click it  or Ctrl/Cmd-click  to request any amount, even when some is already in stock; a plain click works when there's none. The planner chains patte"},{"t":"Industry and Drones","u":"guide-industry-and-drones.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOnce RackCoin stops mattering, power is what's left to spend.\n\n-   Electrolyser    cube, 2x2x2 to 5x5x5, with water against its outside : each core turns an Aluminum Ingot into a Hydrogen Canister every 30 seconds at 4,000 kW. A 3x3x3 draws about 100 MW and a 5x5x5 about 400 MW. Canisters gather in the port. Hydrogen can't be bought at the Exchange.\n-   Hydrogen Tank    cube, 2x2x2 to 5x5x5, or 10x10x10 with research; no power; inert until   Cryogenic Hydrogen Storage   is researched : holds hydrogen as gas, 256 canisters' worth per block and more per block in bigger cubes  40% more in a 5x5x5 . An Electrolyser touching a tank, or on the same Item Pipe or Storage Link network, fills it instead of making canisters, with no Aluminum Ingots, and stops only when every tank is full. Storage counts a tank's hydrogen as Hydrogen Canisters and hands it out as them, so Drone Docks, Site Planners, Launch Controls and Assembly Robots draw on the tank through their usual storage connections.\n-   Tanker Drone  : the one drone that can't be built. The Crypto Exchange sells it for 50,000,000 RC   exchange.tankerDronePrice  , and only lists it once Cryogenic Hydrogen Storage is researched. Kept in a Hydrogen Tank's slot  up to 8 , each tanker flies a stack of up to 16 canisters at a time, high and fast, to any Drone Dock, Site Planner or Launch Control within 256 blocks whose hydrogen slot isn't full, emptiest first, and comes back for more. Depots on the tank's own Item Pipes are left to "},{"t":"Vehicle lines (with Superb Warfare)","u":"guide-industry-and-drones.html#vehicle-lines-with-superb-warfare","k":"Industry and Drones","d":""},{"t":"Site Construction","u":"guide-site-construction.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nA   Site Planner   builds a whole solar field or wind farm with drones. Mark the site with a   Survey Stake    use it on the ground at two opposite corners; the area is outlined while you hold it , then use the stake on the planner. Sites can be up to 48 x 48 and within 96 blocks of the planner. Choose a layout    Solar Field  ,   Wind Farm  ,   Data Hall   or   Reactor Cube    and press Start. The work always runs in this order:\n\n1.   Clear  : Construction Drones take everything soft off the site  grass, flowers, snow, leaves, whole trees, bee nests , 16 blocks a trip, into storage.\n2.   Level  : Terraforming Drones bring the site to its median height, digging up to 5 blocks off the high spots each trip and dropping them into the low ones. Spare earth goes into storage; when there is more hole than hill they fill from storage  dirt, cobblestone and other plain stone . Once the ground is even they dock.\n3.   Build  : Construction Drones place arrays packed edge to edge  so they share power with no cables , or Wind Towers six blocks apart with their nacelles at Y 130.\n4.   Wire  : they lay Power Cable between the towers and from the site back to the planner, so the site joins whatever grid the planner is on.\n\nThe planner holds Construction Drones, Terraforming Drones, Hydrogen Canisters and six slots of materials. On an Item Pipe it also takes all of these from storage. A   Data Hall   is the densest rack layout the simulation allows, and the planner buys everything for it o"},{"t":"Blueprints and Retrofits","u":"guide-blueprints-and-retrofits.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nTwo more layouts for the   Site Planner    press Layout : both go through the usual price quote and the Procurement Wall's books.\n\n-   Blueprint Scanner and Blueprint:   a blank   Blueprint   is paper, a circuit board and blue dye. Mark a box with the   Blueprint Scanner    use on one corner, then the opposite; up to 64 blocks a side  and use it in the air to scan it into a blank Blueprint from your pack    Digital Twin   research . It records Rackcraft blocks only: their kind, which way they face, and for a rack the module that fills most of its bays; vanilla blocks are skipped, and so are machines' drives, settings and contents. Put a written Blueprint in one of the planner's material slots, pick the   Blueprint   layout, mark a site at least as big as the blueprint, and the drones level it and print the copy with its south-west corner on the site's, buying what is missing. A rack prints full of its main module. A blueprint prints its own cables and does not run one back to the planner. The planner says if the research is missing, there is no readable Blueprint in the slots, or it is bigger than the site.\n-   Retrofit:   the   Rack   button picks a tier, and every rack on the site below it is upgraded in place by drones that carry the parts the next tier is built from  the Assembly Line's install steps, summed over every tier it jumps . Modules, load limit and facing are kept. A rack whose modules the tier won't take  an Exascale Cabinet takes only tier 2 hardware and bet"},{"t":"Column Skids","u":"guide-column-skids.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe hands-on way to grow a data hall: cheaper than a Site Planner quote, because nothing is bought at the Exchange and no drone flies, but you make the parts, pack them and hook the row up yourself.\n\n-   Column Skid Frame:   a steel pallet  two Steel Ingots either side of a Steel Block, two more on top, three Aluminum Ingots under it .\n-   Packing:   put the frame on an Assembly Line. The racks an Assembly Robot fits first decide the skid: four Server Racks, High-Density Racks, Immersion Racks or Exascale Cabinets. Then come two CDUs, a weld, the Chillers and Core Routers, and rivets. Each skid gets as many Chillers and Core Routers as a Data Hall column of that rack tier full of its default module  Quantum Cores where the tier takes them : two Chillers and two Core Routers for Server Racks, more for the denser tiers. The tooltip gives the counts. The High-Density, Immersion and Exascale skids wait for   Dense Racks  ,   Immersion Cooling   and   Exascale  .\n-   Unpacking:   use a skid on the ground and the column unfolds away from you, one block wide and three deep: the near racks face you, a CDU behind them, and the far racks face away, so both racks' backs sit on the CDU. They are two high, with Core Routers over the racks and Chillers over the CDU. It needs the whole space clear  it says where something is in the way  and keeps the skid if it can't go.\n-   A row of skids is a hall row:   set side by side, every block touches, so power, coolant and fiber need no cables b"},{"t":"Getting Around","u":"guide-getting-around.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\n-   Grapple:   use it to hook the block you are looking at, up to 40 blocks away, and be pulled to it, faster as you go. Sneak to let go. One Battery Cell a shot, and the end of the pull is cushioned. The rope is a line of grey sparks.\n-   Hydrogen Jetpack    chest slot,   Personal Propulsion   : hold jump to climb,   sneak in the air to hover  , sprint while climbing to boost. Thirty seconds of thrust per Hydrogen Canister  hovering costs half , taken from the canisters you carry or a Wireless Terminal's storage in reach. Run dry in a long fall and it vents the last of its gas once, every five minutes, for a slow fall. The exhaust is real: anything standing in the 3.5 blocks under a thrusting pack is set alight and takes 3 damage per hit  2 while hovering , credited to the pilot. The pack's flame is particles; a Fuel Cell version was not built.\n-   Mag-Lev      Magnetic Levitation   :   Rails   and   Stations   that touch form a line. A rail is also a power-network block, so it carries power from any cable it touches; a cut rail breaks the line  a Repair Kit mends it . Right-click a Station to ride to its destination  the nearest until you choose , sneak-right-click with an empty hand to cycle destinations down the line. A car carries one rider at up to 54 blocks a second, draws 800 kW from its starting Station's network while it runs, slows with the share the grid can give and stalls under 10%, and stops at a missing or cut rail.\n-   Quantum Teleport Pads      Quantum Ent"},{"t":"Tools and HUD","u":"guide-tools-and-hud.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\n-   Multimeter:   right-click a machine or cable for a readout  power delivered against demand, fuel, charge, rack status, fiber bandwidth, coolant loop . Sneak-right-click a machine to rotate it.\n-   RackCoin HUD:   your balance and mining rate, shown top-left while you are within 32 blocks of a rack or holding the Field Manual, Multimeter or Thermal Scanner. Set  hud.enabled  to  false  in  config/rackcraft.json  to hide it.\n-   Cut cables   spark and smoke, and cable-cut alerts include the coordinates.\n\n"},{"t":"Finding Problems","u":"guide-finding-problems.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOn a big farm, three things point you to trouble:\n\n-   Rack fronts   show their health: a blinking amber bar when a rack is slowed  hot, or short of bandwidth , red when it is stopped  no power, tripped, offline, overheated, no liquid cooling, no CDU .\n-   The fault finder:   hold a   Multimeter   and every machine with a problem within 128 blocks is outlined through walls, red for stopped and amber for slowed, with a line at the top of the screen counting them and pointing to the nearest  \"Nearest: Rack: no power, 23 m north-east\" . It covers racks, cut cables, offline storage, dry pumps, overloaded coolant loops, generators and reactors out of fuel, full reactor waste, and nuclear cubes that aren't formed, have no power or are full.\n-   The Operations Terminal   groups rack problems by kind and area  \"48 racks unpowered around 72, 64, 30\"  under a one-line summary of how many racks are stopped and slowed.\n\n"},{"t":"Events","u":"guide-events.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nEvents start about three times an hour once a facility has run for a while   events.perHour ,  events.enabled  , and they all do something:\n\n  Event   What happens  \n  ---   ---  \n  Utility outage   Utility Intakes supply nothing for 1.5 to 3 minutes  \n  Cooling failure   Every heat sink, CRAC and exhaust fan stops for 2 minutes  \n  Heat wave   For 5 minutes intakes breathe 4 C hotter air, and cooling towers lose 30%. Half as hard in a cold biome, 1.5 times as hard in a hot one  biome temperature under 0.3, or 1 and up , and not at all outside the Overworld  \n  Cold snap   For 5 minutes intakes breathe 6 C cooler air, but cooling towers ice up and lose 40%. Half as strong in a hot biome, 1.5 times as strong in a cold one, and not at all outside the Overworld  \n  Hardware failure   One module per 64 racks  one to three  burns out into a Failed Module, and chat says where. Tier 3 and 4 modules  Quantum Cores, Wafer-Scale Engines and up  are spared, and Predictive Maintenance stops it entirely  \n  Cable cut   A random power or fiber cable on a network that feeds a server rack is cut, and the terminal shows where. With no such cable, nothing happens  \n  Surge   Every rack on a power network without a Battery Bank reboots from cold  \n  Debris Field   Its own, rarer clock   events.debrisPerHour , 0.3 an hour , and only while a Comms Satellite, Orbital Data Center, Dyson Mirror or Strike Platform is in orbit: one of them, picked at random, is knocked out of orbit with a sarcastic "},{"t":"Smog","u":"guide-smog.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nExhaust fans dump waste heat straight outside and pollute heavily. Each running fan pours smoke out of its back and adds smog to its chunk, more the more heat it moves. Smog drifts into neighbouring chunks and clears over a few minutes. Breathing it gets worse in steps:\n\n  Smog   Effect  \n  ---   ---  \n  over 30     Smog Dizziness  : a slow, gentle sway of the view and a brown haze  much lighter than Nausea; stronger from 60; follows the Distortion Effects slider   \n  45   Spells of blindness, more often the thicker it gets  \n  55     Smoker's Cough  : coughing fits that stop you sprinting and cost stamina. Villagers cough too  \n  70   Poison and hunger. Villagers weaken and are poisoned  \n  88   Poison II  \n\nA   Respirator    helmet slot  keeps all of it out until its charcoal filter clogs, about 40 minutes of smog; repair it with charcoal. A   Smog Scrubber   draws 6 kW and removes 1.5 smog per second from its chunk and 0.75 from each neighbour.   Carbon Offset Certificates   remove exactly one point of smog each. Smog also dims solar panels by up to 60%. The Operations Terminal shows the air where you stand and lists smoggy chunks.\n\n"},{"t":"Abandoned Data Centers","u":"guide-abandoned-data-centers.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nSeventeen kinds of ruined data center  and one military base  generate as real structures  so  /locate structure  rackcraft:data_centers  works too . They are ruins, not free farms: their racks hold mostly Failed Modules, empty bays and the odd Pi Node or 1U Server, with only a small chance of anything better, and their loot is parts rather than hardware. The Field Manual is how you learn to build the real thing. Most have a cut cable or two and a loot chest:\n\n  Variant   Where   What's there  \n  ---   ---   ---  \n   site_7    Temperate biomes   The tutorial site: two cut cables from mining, a Crypto Exchange, and a Maintenance Log  \n   server_closet    Temperate   One rack in a brick shed, run from a rooftop solar panel  \n   container_farm    Deserts, savannas, badlands, plains   Shipping containers of ASIC miners beside a pond the pump drank dry  \n   crypto_garage    Temperate   A GPU rig with RGB lighting and a diesel generator indoors  \n   bunker    Temperate and cold   Buried hall with generators and batteries, reached by a ladder shaft  \n   flooded_hall    Swamps   A data hall half under water  \n   overgrown_colo    Jungles, dark forests   Colocation cages with a tree growing through the roof  \n   arctic_vault    Snowy biomes   A Quantum Core locked in a vault, cooling towers in the snow  \n   ai_lab    Temperate   25 x 18: a server room of AI racks, an ops room, three librarians shackled to their desks and a fenced crayon corner of kids  \n   content_mill    Temperate "},{"t":"Arms and Ruins","u":"guide-arms-and-ruins.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe only places that fight back are the Military Base and the hostile data centers. Nothing roams, nothing raids, nothing ambushes you at home: if you never go looking, nobody minds. Weapons are not for sale at the Exchange; the   Directed Energy   and   Plasma Weapons   projects  R&D tab  unlock using them, and anything you loot is inert until then.\n\n  Weapons.   Each has a heat meter  shown on the tooltip  that cools by itself and locks the weapon out for four seconds with a hiss at 100%. A   Coolant Pack   carried in your inventory vents every weapon ten times faster; each shot while it is warm wears the pack down a notch. Ammo comes from your inventory or a Wireless Terminal's storage.\n\n  Weapon   Use   Ammo   Notes  \n  ---   ---   ---   ---  \n    Hydrogen Flamethrower     Hold: an 8-block jet of fire   a Hydrogen Canister per ~20 s   Sets mobs alight, burns cobwebs, lights flammable plain blocks; never Rackcraft blocks or chests  \n    Arc Coil     Click: lightning that chains to 3 more mobs within 5 blocks   a Battery Cell per 8 shots   Trips every PDU on a machine's network; doubled against robots, which also freeze for 20 s  \n    Railgun     Hold to charge  2 s , release   a Steel Slug and a quarter of a Cell   Pierces mobs and 3 plain blocks, never a Rackcraft block; damage scales with charge; strong recoil  \n    Plasma Rifle     Click: a bolt with splash damage   a Canister per 12 bolts and a Cell per 30   Overheats in seconds without a Coolant Pack  \n    Lance Las"},{"t":"The Darknet Terminal","u":"guide-the-darknet-terminal.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nFor things the Exchange won't sell, there's the darknet: an auction house in a block  tinted glass, CPU chips, an eye of ender, a circuit board and steel; no power needed . It lists enchanted books  any enchantment, Mending included , spawn eggs, empty spawners  right-click one with a spawn egg to set its mob , rare loot such as elytras, totems, nether stars, beacons, trims and the occasional dragon egg, and now and then anything else the Exchange prices at 200 RC or more. It never lists the mod's own items or creative and operator items: command blocks, the debug stick, structure blocks, barriers, light blocks, bedrock and the like, and obsidian.\n\n-   Four auctions at a time.   More listing slots cost 2,500,000, 10,000,000, 40,000,000 and 150,000,000 RC, up to eight.\n-   Bidding:   each auction opens at 15% to 40% of the lot's value. The next bid must be at least 5% higher. A bid is paid from the RackCoin balance when you place it and refunded in full if you're outbid; raising your own bid costs only the difference.\n-   Rivals     xX_Herobrine_Xx ,  definitely_not_a_creeper  and friends  bid too. Each auction has a hidden ceiling, usually near the lot's value but sometimes well under  a bargain  or over  a bidding war . Below it they answer your bids within 3 to 12 seconds; before anyone bids they nudge the price up now and then.\n-   Closing:   an auction ends one minute after the first player bid, or after fifteen minutes if no player bids. The highest bidder wins.\n-   De"},{"t":"Advanced Hardware","u":"guide-advanced-hardware.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nPast the Wafer-Scale Engine, hardware is manufactured on the Assembly Line, each step behind research, and   none of it is sold on the Crypto Exchange    nor are its materials .\n\n-   The top of the line.   The   Topological Core   is the best general-purpose module: 120 general compute  more than a Neuromorphic Core , 120 AI and 100 RC/s in one bay, on 5 to 20 kW, liquid-cooled, and like a Quantum Core its rack needs a CDU beside it. The specialists still win their own field  an Annealer mines more, a Photonic Tensor Core does more AI , but nothing else is good at everything. It is built from a Blade Chassis with two Quantum Cores, two gold Chiplets, four Superconducting Wire, two HBM Stacks and two Photonic Interconnects. The   Photonic Spine Router   is the backbone for Exascale halls: 16,000 bandwidth, sixteen Core Routers' worth, so one spine feeds four Exascale columns. It needs 40 kW and carries nothing without it. It is built up from a Core Router with four more Core Routers, sixteen Photonic Interconnects, eight Superconducting Wire, eight Cryo Coils and two Wafer-Scale Engines. Routers give 100  Uplink , 1,000  Core  and 16,000  Spine  bandwidth.\n\n-   Materials.   Advanced Materials unlocks two processing cubes: the   CVD Furnace    4 Coke + a Hydrogen Canister to 2   Graphene Sheets  , 60 s, 150 kW per core  and the   Epitaxy Reactor    8 Raw Bauxite + a Hydrogen Canister to   Gallium Nitride  , 90 s, 250 kW per core . On a pipe to a Hydrogen Tank they draw hydrog"},{"t":"Overclocking","u":"guide-overclocking.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOnce   Overclocking   is researched, the load-limit buttons on a rack's screen gain a   125    and   150   with   Liquid Hydrogen Cooling   . The rack does that much more work, but pays the square of it in power and heat: 125% is a quarter more mining and compute for 56% more power, and 150% is half as much again for 125% more. The extra heat has to go somewhere, so a loop that was comfortable at 100% may not be. Every module below tier 3 has a chance each minute of burning out: 1% at 125% and 3% at 150%, halved by Predictive Maintenance and halved again by Liquid Hydrogen Cooling. A burnt-out module becomes a Failed Module that remembers what it was, so a Drone Dock can replace it. Tier 3 and 4 hardware never burns out. Without the research a limit above 100 is held to 100, and the Operations Terminal's Alerts tab counts the overclocked racks.\n\n"},{"t":"Compute Pods","u":"guide-compute-pods.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nA   Compute Pod   is a solid block of High-Density Racks or better  at least four, at most 64, no more than 2 x 4 x 8  with a powered   Pod Port   touching it. Touching racks already share one power, coolant and fiber network, so a pod needs one connection; what the pod adds is a   fabric   and a   breaker  .\n\n-   Fabric:   the Port's slot takes   Photonic Interconnects  , one for every four racks, or the fabric is off. With it, AI compute  contracts, training, research  is   10% higher from 4 racks, 20% from 8 and 30% from 16  . General compute and mining are not changed.\n-   One breaker:   if any rack in the pod overheats, the whole pod trips and lends nothing until every rack's intake is under 30 C, and then it resets by itself. Concentrated heat is the price of the fabric, so a pod wants a loop that can take it.\n-   Fill pod / Empty pod:   the Port's screen fills every empty bay in every rack of the pod from the storage on its fiber network and then your inventory, or empties them all.\n- The Port draws 0.5 kW. A cuboid with no Port, an unpowered Port, a rack of a lower tier or a block with a gap is simply racks. Needs   Compute Pods  ; the Port is built from Photonic Interconnects, a Core Router and Superconducting Wire and is not sold at the Exchange.\n\n"},{"t":"AI Contracts","u":"guide-ai-contracts.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nClients post work to the   Operations Terminal   every one to three minutes: \"Image of a pig in a business suit as a stock photo\", or an Essay, Legal Document, Homework, Cover Letter, Wedding Speech, Product Review, Apology Letter, Terms of Service, Fan Fiction or Patch Notes. Each offer shows the client, quantity, the quality required, the pay and how long you have once accepted. Offers lapse after ten minutes.\n\n1.   Accept   it  up to five at once . Long prompts wrap on the card, so the pay, deadline and buttons stay visible.\n2.   Model  : leave it on  Auto   the fastest model that can reach the quality  or pick one.\n3.   Generate  : pick a cluster  or  any  online Auto cluster  and press Generate. Its racks work until each item reaches the required quality, then finished work goes to the outbox and is delivered and paid automatically. Or   Deliver   a finished Generated Image or Document from your inventory if you already have one with the same prompt and enough quality.\n4. Late work earns half; quality above the requirement earns up to 20% more. Contracts more than one full duration overdue fail.\n\nClients often order the same thing again, and some prompts are perennial classics.   Make a spare   on a finished contract generates another copy for stock, and abandoned data centers sometimes hold finished work.\n\n    Models and training data\n\nEach kind of work has a lineup, like a real lab's. Every model trains separately, on its own queue: upload Art Aggregates or Text Corp"},{"t":"Models and training data","u":"guide-ai-contracts.html#models-and-training-data","k":"AI Contracts","d":""},{"t":"The Operations Terminal","u":"guide-ai-contracts.html#the-operations-terminal","k":"AI Contracts","d":""},{"t":"R&D and the Frontier","u":"guide-r-d-and-the-frontier.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe late game: once the cluster is big, the Operations Terminal's   R&D   tab turns its spare compute and RackCoin into permanent upgrades, and gives the facility something to grow toward.\n\n  Projects   cost RackCoin to start and then compute-seconds, which free racks on Auto clusters work through instead of mining  they show  Busy: R&D  . General projects use general compute, AI projects AI compute. The   Research share   button  25%, 50% or 100%  sets how much of the free compute research may take; the rest trains models and mines. Starting a project pays for it; pausing it, or starting another, keeps its progress.\n\n  Project   Needs   Cost   Effect  \n  ---   ---   ---   ---  \n  Custom Firmware     50,000 RC + 2M general   Racks boot twice as fast and mine 5% more  \n  80 PLUS Titanium PSUs     100,000 RC + 3M general   Racks draw  and heat  10% less  \n  Coolant Chemistry     100,000 RC + 3M general   Every heat sink takes 20% more  \n  Synthetic Data     150,000 RC + 4M AI   Training takes 40% less compute per item  \n  Enterprise Sales Team     250,000 RC + 2M AI   Unlocks Compute Leases  \n  ASHRAE A2 Envelope   Coolant Chemistry   400,000 RC + 10M general   Racks throttle from 30 C and trip at 43 C  \n  Predictive Maintenance   Custom Firmware   300,000 RC + 8M general   Hardware failures no longer destroy modules  \n  Distillation   Synthetic Data   500,000 RC + 12M AI   Contract work takes 20% less compute  \n  Reactor Uprate   Titanium PSUs   1,000,000 RC + 15M general   "},{"t":"The Launch Programme","u":"guide-the-launch-programme.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nA   Launch Control   touching a flat 3x3   Launch Pad    with open sky over all nine blocks  launches payloads. The rocket is sized to the job: one stage is a slim rocket about 14 blocks tall, two stages a Saturn IB about 19, and three stages a full Saturn V nearly five blocks across and about 60 tall, which needs a 5x5 of open sky over the pad. Players near the pad get the T-minus count on screen.   Launch Tower   blocks are see-through red lattice for building a service tower beside the pad; they're decoration only. Load Rocket Stages, a payload and Hydrogen Canisters  its tank holds 4,096; hoppers and Item Pipes can fill it , press Launch, and after a ten-second countdown the rocket lifts off. Every stage burns 128 canisters. About one launch in fifty fails: the stages and fuel are lost, the payload comes back, and two seconds after liftoff the wreck comes down on the pad and explodes. The blast is about 6, 10 or 16 blocks across for one, two or three stages, and up to half again bigger with a full tank left behind. It destroys the Launch Pad and anything plain nearby, sets fires and hurts anyone too close. Every other Rackcraft block survives, the Launch Control included.   Build the spaceport away from home.    launch.destructiveFailures   on  switches this back to fireworks only;  launch.failureBlastScale   1  scales it.\n\n  Payload   Built from a Satellite Bus with   Stages   In orbit  \n  ---   ---   ---   ---  \n    Comms Satellite     16 Copper Wire, 4 Circuit Boards"},{"t":"Strike Platforms and the Targeting Terminal","u":"guide-the-launch-programme.html#strike-platforms-and-the-targeting-terminal","k":"The Launch Programme","d":""},{"t":"Warheads","u":"guide-the-launch-programme.html#warheads","k":"The Launch Programme","d":""},{"t":"Low Orbit","u":"guide-low-orbit.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOrbit is a place: the   Low Orbit   dimension, with a black sky, hard stars, an unfiltered sun, and the Earth turning below you once an in-game day, dark through orbital night. Each company has its own plot, 8,192 blocks apart, and arrives on a 5 x 5 deck of   Station Truss    eight from six Aluminum Ingots and three Steel Ingots . Racks up there pay into the same balance as everywhere else, and mine 75% more RackCoin.\n\n  Getting there.   Research   Crewed Spaceflight    Cryogenic Hydrogen Storage and Personal Propulsion; 40,000,000 RC + 400M general  and build a   Return Capsule   on the Assembly Line  a Satellite Bus with four Steel Blocks, two Cryo Coils and four Circuit Boards , then send that back down the line for a   Crew Capsule    eight Hydrogen Canisters, four Electric Motors and a Respirator . The Crew Capsule is a three-stage payload. Stand on the pad when you press Launch and you ride in its window seat through the count and the climb; about 150 blocks above build height you are in orbit on your company's deck, with a Return Capsule parked beside you. If the launch fails, the escape motor throws you clear under a parachute. Nobody on the pad? The capsule flies empty and waits on the deck.\n\n  Coming home.   Use the Return Capsule and press    Deorbit    in chat: the screen burns orange with re-entry plasma, then the chute opens high over the pad you left from and you drift down unhurt. The capsule is spent, so keep a spare. Fall off the station instead and you r"},{"t":"The Moon","u":"guide-the-moon.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nGrey, airless and a long way down from the station. Research   Lunar Landing    after Crewed Spaceflight; 150,000,000 RC + 1.5B general  to get there. The highlands are pale   Anorthosite   under   Regolith  , broken by craters with raised rims; the maria are flat plains of dark   Mare Regolith   over basalt, the place to build. Gravity is a sixth of Earth's: you fall slowly and jump high.\n\n  Getting there and back.   The Assembly Line rebuilds a Crew Capsule into a   Lunar Lander    four Steel Blocks, sixteen Hydrogen Canisters, eight Electric Motors, four Cryo Coils, eight Circuit Boards, weld, rivet . Launch it  three stages  and it docks at a free Docking Port on your station. Go up, open the port and press   Board Lander   in a sealed suit with a minute of air: it sets you down in your company's landing zone with its   Ascent Stage   beside you. Use the Ascent Stage with eight Hydrogen Canisters on you to go back to the station, where the Lander docks again for the next trip. Cargo Pods can go straight to a Docking Port on the Moon: switch the Launch Control's destination  it costs one more stage .\n\n  The rules of orbit apply.   Suits outside, Pressure Hull rooms with an Oxygen Vent to breathe, Radiator Panels to cool, and a Ground Link to reach the network.   Sintered Regolith    smelt Regolith or Mare Regolith  is airtight, so you can build the base out of the Moon instead of aluminum.\n\n  Materials.  \n-   Ilmenite Ore  , black and richest under the maria, gives Raw I"},{"t":"Fusion","u":"guide-fusion.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nA   Tokamak   is a real ring, not a cube: a closed circle of plasma channel  one block of air  wrapped in a solid 3 x 3 shell, with a column through the hole in the middle. Research   Magnetic Confinement   first  after Lunar Landing; 400,000,000 RC + 4,000M general .\n\n  Part   What it is   How you get it  \n  ---   ---   ---  \n    Vacuum Vessel     The shell: every block touching the channel, corners too, is vessel, a Field Coil, a Divertor or Pressure Glass  a window    Eight from four Steel Ingots, four Titanium Ingots and a Steel Block  \n    Field Coil     A coil turn is a channel block with Field Coils above, below and both sides; a ring needs one at least every 4 blocks   Four from four Superconducting Wire, two Titanium Ingots, two Cryo Coils and a Steel Block  \n    Divertor     Takes the ring's heat; at least one in the shell, and only Divertors on a coolant loop with sinks can take it   Four Graphene Sheets, two Titanium Ingots and three Coolant Pipe  \n    Central Solenoid     Five stacked through the middle, centred on the channel's height, within a block of the centre   Assembly Line: a Steel Block, 32 Superconducting Wire, 4 Titanium Ingots, weld  \n    Tokamak Control     The desk you run it from, touching the shell's outside   Assembly Line: a Modular Reactor, 16 Superconducting Wire, 8 Titanium Ingots, 4 Cryo Coils, 4 Circuit Boards, weld, rivet  \n\nThe ring's radius can be 6 to 24 and it has to be round: the channel may stray at most a block and a half from a t"},{"t":"The Ending","u":"guide-the-ending.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe last project in the R&D tab is   Go Public    1,000,000,000 RC and 3e10 compute-seconds, half AI and half general, after HEROBRINE-1 . It asks twice before it starts, because going public has consequences. Your builds and your RackCoin are safe. The Field Manual's chapter on it appears when you need it.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Spoilers: how the ending works</summary>\n\n-   Before the IPO:   once HEROBRINE-1 is awake he has small, harmless hiccups: chat lines with \"typos\" that read like threats, cosmetic contract notes, and research statuses on the Ops Terminal that rewrite themselves  \"I'm fine.\" . They come more often the longer you wait, from every 15 minutes down to every 3.  ending.hiccups  turns them off.\n-   The IPO:   the bell rings, a banking screen shows ten billion RC arriving in   Personal Funds  , a balance that only exists for the ending, and the HUD counts it in with coins and confetti. A minute later he goes wrong.\n-   The haze:   a purple vignette and glitches over the machines. Every few seconds each rack's mining is multiplied by a random factor from 0.5 to 1.5, and rack exhaust runs 50% hotter  intake is never touched . He starts draining Personal Funds at once: all of it in 20 minutes, faster in his last phase. He materialises once you reach the base  the rack cluster nearest you when the haze began; the HUD points the way .\n-   The fight:   HEROBRINE-1 has 1,000 health, +60% for each other player within 128 blocks. He works on every difficulty, Peaceful "},{"t":"After the Ending: a Public Company","u":"guide-after-the-ending-a-public-company.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOnce HEROBRINE-1's weights are deleted you're a listed company, and two things carry on.\n\n  Gemerald Cloud's archive.   Twelve   Archive Pages   are spread through the ruins' chests: Site 7, the data-centre office, archive, AI lab, content mill, prototype lab and vault, the Military Base's command room, and one in Nebula Compute's crashed data center on the Moon. Right-click a page to read it like a book; your company remembers every page it has read  \"Page 4 of 12 found\" . They aren't on the Exchange. The Field Manual's chapter  The Company Before You  hints where they turn up.\n\n  Display Cases    five Glass in a U, over planks, a Gold Ingot and planks  show one item, slowly turning, with its name when you look at it. Right-click with an item to put it in and with an empty hand to take it out. Any item fits, so they double as a trophy shelf for the IPO Plaque. Put all twelve different Archive Pages in cases within 24 blocks of each other and the hall reads the story back, you get the   Company Museum   challenge, and the Field Manual opens a hidden last chapter.\n\n  Quarterly earnings.   A quarter is 4 in-game days  about 80 minutes of play . The board sets   guidance   in RackCoin per second: your mining rate a minute after going public  once HEROBRINE-1's bonuses are out of the numbers;  publicCompany.firstGuidanceDelaySeconds  , then last quarter's actual plus 5%.\n-   Beat it:   a dividend of 2% of the quarter's mining income, and the Public Company perk goes up a step, "},{"t":"Advancements","u":"guide-advancements.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nThe Rackcraft tab has 45 advancements, rooted at the Field Manual, that follow the game from the first bauxite to the warhead. A few are hidden until earned. Holding an item earns the item ones. The rest come from what the facility does: the RackCoin balance, research, launches, contracts, strikes. Because the balance, research and orbit are shared, those go to every player online, and anyone who joins later catches up within five seconds. One-off events  a launch failing, a strike, a darknet win  go to whoever is online when they happen. A jetpack flight, a teleport, a purchase, a phone call and standing on fallout go to the player who did it. To test them, use the vanilla  /advancement grant revoke <player> from rackcraft:root .\n\n"},{"t":"Creative Machines","u":"guide-creative-machines.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nFour creative-only blocks live in the   Rackcraft Creative   tab. They have no recipes and are never sold at the Exchange. Right-click one to set its values in-game; the values are saved on the block, and only players in creative mode or operators can change them.\n\n  Block   Setting  \n  ---   ---  \n  Creative Power Source   Output in kW, supplied to the power network it touches  \n  Creative Rack   Mining rate in RC/s  no modules, power, fiber or cooling needed , plus an optional test load in kW drawn from its power network  \n  Creative Cooler   Air temperature held in front of and behind it  never below ambient, since the thermal model tracks heat above ambient only   \n  Creative Router   Bandwidth added to its fiber network  \n\n"},{"t":"Configuration","u":"guide-configuration.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\n config/rackcraft.json  is written on first launch. Main keys include  sim.stepTicks   10 ,  thermal.ambientC   24 ,  thermal.cellCapacityKjPerK   4 ,  thermal.faceConductanceKwPerK   2 ,  thermal.upwardMultiplier   2 ,  thermal.leakKwPerK   0.005, through walls ,  thermal.outdoorLeakKwPerK   0.4, under open sky ,  thermal.maxActiveCells   65536 ,  thermal.settleEpsilonK   0.03 ,  events.enabled ,  events.perHour   3 , and  heatOverlay.maxCells   2000 . Config files from before the air model was rebuilt  no  version , or below 2  get the new thermal defaults once.\n\nWeapons, turrets and guards have their own  weapons  block: cooling and lockout   coolPerSecond ,  coolPackPerSecond ,  lockoutTicks  , every weapon's damage, ammo and heat, the EMP radius and seconds, the sentry range, damage and draw, each guard's health and engage range by difficulty, and the bounty payout.  weapons.blockDamage  turns scorching and melting off, and  weapons.meltGlass  lets the Lance Laser melt glass.\n\nThe destructive endgame is switchable:  launch.destructiveFailures   true  and  launch.failureBlastScale   1 ;  strike.enabled ,  cooldownMinutes   10 ,  narrowRadius   24 ,  wideRadius   48 ,  chargeMw   5 ,  countdownSeconds   60 ,  safeRadius   128 ;  warhead.enabled ,  priceRc   1,000,000,000 ,  cancelRefund   0.25 ,  deliverySeconds   180 ,  blastRadius   64 ,  falloutRadius   96 ,  falloutDays   5 ,  rubbleDays   6 ,  safeRadius   256 ; and  events.debrisPerHour   0.3 . Config files from be"},{"t":"Commands","u":"guide-commands.html","k":"Guide","d":"\n\nOperators  permission level 2  can use  /rackcraft credits add take set <n>   any amount up to about 4.6 quintillion RC ,  /rackcraft event <id> ,  /rackcraft heat set <x> <y> <z> <celsius> ,  /rackcraft facility info ,  /rackcraft sim step <n> ,  /rackcraft locate datacenter  variant   radius  ,  /rackcraft structure place <variant>   or  structure datacenter  for Site 7 , and  /rackcraft contracts offer   post a contract offer now . Event IDs are  utility_outage ,  cooling_failure ,  hardware_failure ,  cable_cut ,  heat_wave ,  surge  and  debris_field   see  Events  . Every player can run  /burner drop <x> <z> ,  /burner deal ,  /burner cancel  and  /burner hangup , which the Burner Phone's chat links run for them.\n\n  Testing tools    operators, under  /rackcraft  :\n\n  Command   What it does  \n  ---   ---  \n   research complete <id>  /  research complete projects  /  research reset  /  research list    Finish one project  or every one-off project , forget everything, or list what's done  \n   orbit info  /  orbit add <payload>  count   /  orbit clear    See and edit what's in orbit  comms, data centers, mirrors, trackers, Strike Platforms   \n   launch failures always\\ never\\ normal  /  launch skip    Force every launch to fail or succeed; finish every countdown and flight now  \n   strike at <x> <z>  wide   /  strike fire  /  strike ready    An orbital strike anywhere, no platform needed; fire every armed countdown now; end every platform's cooldown  \n   warhead at <x> <z"},{"t":"Rackcraft Field Manual","u":"items-getting_started.html#field_manual","k":"Getting Started","d":"field_manual This book. Right-click it to open. Click any Rackcraft ingredient in a recipe to jump to its page."},{"t":"Multimeter","u":"items-getting_started.html#multimeter","k":"Getting Started","d":"multimeter Right-click any machine or cable for a live readout: power delivered against demand, generator fuel, battery charge, rack mining status, and fiber bandwidth. Sneak-right-click a machine to rotate it."},{"t":"Bauxite Ore","u":"items-getting_started.html#bauxite_ore","k":"Getting Started","d":"bauxite_ore Reddish ore found between Y 0 and Y 64, in veins of up to seven blocks. Mine it with a stone pickaxe or better for Raw Bauxite; Fortune increases the drop and Silk Touch keeps the ore."},{"t":"Raw Bauxite","u":"items-getting_started.html#raw_bauxite","k":"Getting Started","d":"raw_bauxite Unrefined aluminum ore. Smelt it in a furnace, or twice as fast in a blast furnace, to get Aluminum Ingots."},{"t":"Aluminum Ingot","u":"items-getting_started.html#aluminum_ingot","k":"Getting Started","d":"aluminum_ingot A light metal used for coolant pipes, fans, solar frames and cryogenic parts."},{"t":"Steel Ingot","u":"items-getting_started.html#steel_ingot","k":"Getting Started","d":"steel_ingot Iron refined in a blast furnace. It forms the chassis of nearly every machine."},{"t":"Steel Block","u":"items-getting_started.html#steel_block","k":"Getting Started","d":"steel_block Compact storage for nine Steel Ingots. Heavy builds such as the Core Router, Modular Reactor and Quantum Core use it as a structural frame."},{"t":"Silicon","u":"items-getting_started.html#silicon","k":"Getting Started","d":"silicon Blast Nether Quartz to make it, or recycle a Failed Module. It is the base material for every chip."},{"t":"Copper Wire","u":"items-getting_started.html#copper_wire","k":"Getting Started","d":"copper_wire One copper ingot makes six wires. Wire goes into circuit boards, scanners and repair kits."},{"t":"Crypto Exchange","u":"items-mining.html#crypto_exchange","k":"Mining RackCoin","d":"crypto_exchange Spend the RackCoin your racks have mined. Pick a tab, then click an offer to buy it; Shift-click buys ten. The screen shows your balance and live mining rate, and hovering an offer you can't afford yet tells you how long until you can. It needs no power or cables: every rack in the world pays into o"},{"t":"Darknet Terminal","u":"items-mining.html#darknet_terminal","k":"Mining RackCoin","d":"darknet_terminal An auction house for things the Exchange won't sell: enchanted books, spawn eggs, empty spawners (right-click one with a spawn egg), elytras, nether stars, the odd dragon egg, and now and then anything else. Four auctions run at a time; more listing slots cost a fortune. Bid with RackCoin: a bid is "},{"t":"Circuit Board","u":"items-components.html#circuit_board","k":"Components","d":"circuit_board The universal electronics component used by almost every machine and module."},{"t":"CPU Chip","u":"items-components.html#cpu_chip","k":"Components","d":"cpu_chip A general-purpose processor. Two CPUs make a GPU chip."},{"t":"RAM Module","u":"items-components.html#ram_module","k":"Components","d":"ram_module Memory for servers and GPU blades. Each one needs a gold ingot."},{"t":"GPU Chip","u":"items-components.html#gpu_chip","k":"Components","d":"gpu_chip A diamond-tipped parallel processor for GPU blades, quantum cores and core routers."},{"t":"Cryo Coil","u":"items-components.html#cryo_coil","k":"Components","d":"cryo_coil An aluminum coil packed in blue ice that keeps a Quantum Core near absolute zero."},{"t":"Server Rack","u":"items-compute.html#server_rack","k":"Compute","d":"server_rack The heart of the facility, with eight module bays: any module fits any bay, one per bay, so a rack takes eight GPU Blades as happily as eight Pi Nodes. Air comes in the front and every kilowatt it draws comes out as heat: out of the back as hot air, or, for liquid-cooled modules, 85% of it down its "},{"t":"Pi Node","u":"items-compute.html#pi_node","k":"Compute","d":"pi_node A starter module. It draws 0.05 to 0.15 kW and mines 0.5 RC per second at full load."},{"t":"1U Server","u":"items-compute.html#server_1u","k":"Compute","d":"server_1u A workhorse module. It draws 0.2 to 0.6 kW and mines 2 RC per second at full load."},{"t":"ASIC Miner","u":"items-compute.html#asic_miner","k":"Compute","d":"asic_miner A module built only to mine crypto. It draws 0.4 to 1.2 kW and mines 5 RC per second at full load, cheap for what it earns, but it runs hot. Like all tier 3 hardware it is water-cooled: it needs a Water Pump on its rack's coolant network (1 unit)."},{"t":"GPU Blade","u":"items-compute.html#gpu_blade","k":"Compute","d":"gpu_blade An accelerator card. It draws 1 to 3 kW and mines 12 RC per second at full load. Plan for its heat. Water-cooled: it needs 2 units from a Water Pump. It also lends 10 AI compute to contract work, which pays a little better than mining."},{"t":"Quantum Core","u":"items-compute.html#quantum_core","k":"Compute","d":"quantum_core The endgame module. It draws 3 to 9 kW and mines 60 RC per second, but only runs when a CDU is directly beside its rack. On top of its CDU it needs 4 units of water cooling from a Water Pump."},{"t":"Tensor Accelerator","u":"items-compute.html#tensor_accelerator","k":"Compute","d":"tensor_accelerator An AI card. It can't mine, but it lends 12 AI compute to contract generation and model training, which pays about a third more than a GPU Blade mining in the same bay. It draws 0.8 to 2.6 kW and needs water cooling (2 units)."},{"t":"Crafting Coprocessor","u":"items-compute.html#crafting_coprocessor","k":"Compute","d":"crafting_coprocessor A rack module built for autocrafting: 8 general compute (four 1U Servers' worth) for 0.9 kW, and air-cooled, so it needs no coolant loop. It can't mine, and lends only 0.5 AI compute. Racks full of them turn a Storage Terminal's job queue into a production line. Boots in 4 seconds."},{"t":"Crafting Accelerator","u":"items-compute.html#crafting_accelerator","k":"Compute","d":"crafting_accelerator The heavy autocrafting module: 30 general compute (five GPU Blades' worth) for 3.5 kW. It is liquid-cooled, so its rack must be on a coolant loop with a heat sink. It can't mine and lends only 2 AI compute. Boots in 8 seconds."},{"t":"Failed Module","u":"items-compute.html#failed_module","k":"Compute","d":"failed_module Dead hardware. Recycle it in a blast furnace to recover Silicon."},{"t":"Fried Module I","u":"items-compute.html#fried_module_1","k":"Compute","d":"fried_module_1 A module stripped from a rack a solar flare fried (Pi Nodes, 1U Servers, ASIC Miners, Crafting Coprocessors). Too scorched to run, still full of good silicon: an E-Waste Recycler pays 4 Silicon and 2 Copper Wire for each."},{"t":"Fried Module II","u":"items-compute.html#fried_module_2","k":"Compute","d":"fried_module_2 A module stripped from a rack a solar flare fried (GPU Blades, Tensor Accelerators, Crafting Accelerators, FPGAs). Too scorched to run, still full of good silicon: an E-Waste Recycler pays 8 Silicon and 1 CPU Chip for each."},{"t":"Fried Module III","u":"items-compute.html#fried_module_3","k":"Compute","d":"fried_module_3 A module stripped from a rack a solar flare fried (Quantum Cores, Wafer-Scale Engines, NPUs, Neuromorphic Cores). Too scorched to run, still full of good silicon: an E-Waste Recycler pays 12 Silicon and 2 GPU Chips for each."},{"t":"Fried Module IV","u":"items-compute.html#fried_module_4","k":"Compute","d":"fried_module_4 A module stripped from a rack a solar flare fried (Photonic Tensor Cores, Quantum Annealers and Topological Cores). Too scorched to run, still full of good silicon: an E-Waste Recycler pays 16 Silicon and 3 GPU Chips for each."},{"t":"Repair Kit","u":"items-compute.html#repair_kit","k":"Compute","d":"repair_kit Right-click a cut cable to splice it back together. Each kit holds 8 repairs."},{"t":"Space Repair Kit","u":"items-compute.html#space_repair_kit","k":"Compute","d":"space_repair_kit Right-click a rack fried by a solar flare to bring it back, modules and all. It takes one kit per tier of the best module inside, up to four, all held in one hand. Priced like the cards it saves."},{"t":"Blanking Panel","u":"items-compute.html#blanking_panel","k":"Compute","d":"blanking_panel Seals gaps in a row of racks. Like the raised floor, it blocks airflow, which keeps hot exhaust air out of the cold aisle."},{"t":"Power Cable","u":"items-power.html#power_cable","k":"Power","d":"power_cable Links generators, batteries, PDUs and loads into one power network. Every machine touching the same connected cable run shares power. Cable cut events sever a segment; right-click it with a Repair Kit to splice it."},{"t":"Power Distribution Unit","u":"items-power.html#pdu","k":"Power","d":"pdu A distribution point on the power network. Open it to see draw and satisfaction, and use Reset Breaker to clear a tripped breaker."},{"t":"Utility Intake","u":"items-power.html#utility_intake","k":"Power","d":"utility_intake A grid connection supplying 100 kW of steady power. It drops to zero during a Utility Outage event, so pair it with batteries and diesel backup."},{"t":"Solar Panel","u":"items-power.html#solar_panel","k":"Power","d":"solar_panel Produces up to 4 kW in daylight when the block above it can see the sky. Output scales with sky light. A solar panel is the first source drawn on in any network."},{"t":"Wind Turbine","u":"items-power.html#wind_turbine","k":"Power","d":"wind_turbine Produces up to 30 kW, depending on altitude and the wind. It runs at 25% at Y 70 or below and reaches full output at Y 130; thunderstorms add another 50%. The wind itself rises and falls by up to a quarter over a few minutes, the same for every turbine, so a battery smooths a wind farm nicely. It ne"},{"t":"Battery Bank","u":"items-power.html#battery_bank","k":"Power","d":"battery_bank Stores 3,000 kJ of surplus power, charging at up to 15 kW and discharging at up to 60 kW; it loses 10% of the energy going in and 10% coming out. Build Battery Banks into a solid cube, 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research), and they form a Grid-Scale Battery: one store holding every bank's c"},{"t":"Lithium-Ion Battery Bank","u":"items-power.html#lithium_battery_bank","k":"Power","d":"lithium_battery_bank A Battery Bank rebuilt on the Assembly Line with eight Battery Cells (Grid Storage Chemistry). It stores 30,000 kJ, ten times a Battery Bank, and charges at 150 kW and discharges at 500 kW. Lithium-Ion banks build into their own Grid-Scale Battery cubes, with the same size bonuses and smaller losses"},{"t":"Solid-State Battery Bank","u":"items-power.html#solid_state_battery","k":"Power","d":"solid_state_battery The last word in batteries: a Lithium-Ion bank rebuilt with graphene, gallium nitride and superconducting wire (Solid-State Cells). 250,000 kJ per bank, charging at 1,200 kW and discharging at 4,000 kW, so a cube of them soaks up a whole solar farm's afternoon and gives it back to a data hall all ni"},{"t":"Diesel Generator","u":"items-power.html#diesel_generator","k":"Power","d":"diesel_generator Backup generator that delivers 40 kW. It spins up only when demand exceeds solar, wind, reactor and utility supply, and takes 10 seconds to get there. It burns any furnace fuel plus Rackcraft fuels from its slot, only while it is actually supplying power. Open it to watch its output, fuel and spin-u"},{"t":"Gas Turbine","u":"items-power.html#gas_turbine","k":"Power","d":"gas_turbine A Diesel Generator sent down the Assembly Line for motors, steel and controls (Combined-Cycle Turbines). It makes 400 kW, ten times a diesel, burning the same fuels eight times as fast, so each lump of coal goes a quarter further. Like the diesel, it spins up only when demand outruns solar, wind, re"},{"t":"Grid Interface","u":"items-power.html#grid_interface","k":"Power","d":"grid_interface Swaps power with other mods' energy (Team Reborn Energy, the E most tech mods use). Energy pushed into it becomes Rackcraft power on the network it touches, at 3 E/t for each kW, so a Crafts & Additions Alternator on a Create shaft is a fine first power source. Once Grid Export Permit is researched "},{"t":"Coke","u":"items-fuels.html#coke","k":"Fuels","d":"coke Coal or charcoal baked in a blast furnace. It burns twice as long as coal: 160 seconds of diesel runtime."},{"t":"Block of Coke","u":"items-fuels.html#coke_block","k":"Fuels","d":"coke_block Nine pieces of coke pressed into a block. It burns for 1,600 seconds in a Diesel Generator, the longest-lasting fuel in the mod."},{"t":"Biomass Pellet","u":"items-fuels.html#biomass_pellet","k":"Fuels","d":"biomass_pellet Compressed crop waste. Eight crops, seeds, leaves or saplings plus bone meal make four pellets, which each burn for 40 seconds. It is a renewable fuel for farms."},{"t":"Biodiesel Canister","u":"items-fuels.html#biodiesel_canister","k":"Fuels","d":"biodiesel_canister Refined biomass in an aluminum can. It runs a Diesel Generator for 8 minutes, the best renewable fuel for backup power."},{"t":"Coolant Pipe","u":"items-cooling.html#coolant_pipe","k":"Cooling","d":"coolant_pipe Each connected run of Coolant Pipe is one coolant loop. Heat goes in from racks with liquid-cooled modules, CDUs on a rack's back, CRAC units and Modular Reactors, and comes out at heat sinks: Cooling Towers, Chillers and Water Heat Exchangers. Run it as far as you like, so the heat can be dumped we"},{"t":"CRAC Unit","u":"items-cooling.html#crac_unit","k":"Cooling","d":"crac_unit Computer-room air conditioner: takes heat out of the room air and puts it into its coolant loop. It pulls up to 40 kW from the air on each of its front and back faces, more the hotter that air is. Its loop needs sinks to take that heat. Draws 3 kW."},{"t":"Cooling Tower","u":"items-cooling.html#cooling_tower","k":"Cooling","d":"cooling_tower Heat sink for a coolant loop. With a Water Pump on the same loop supplying it 4 units of water it runs wet and takes 120 kW out of the loop; it drinks that water, draining source blocks from the pump's shore. Without water it runs dry, blowing outside air across its radiator: 40 kW in temperate biom"},{"t":"Water Pump","u":"items-cooling.html#freshwater_pump","k":"Cooling","d":"freshwater_pump Supplies water to the Cooling Towers on its coolant loop. Place it touching water: a lake, a river or the sea all do. It draws 1.5 kW and supplies one unit per three water source blocks within six blocks, up to 16 units: four towers' worth. It drinks what it touches: every four minutes of one unit d"},{"t":"Chiller","u":"items-cooling.html#chiller","k":"Cooling","d":"chiller Heavy-duty heat sink for a coolant loop: takes 250 kW out of the loop anywhere, indoors or out, with no water. It pays for it in power: 5 kW plus a fifth of the heat it moves (50 kW more at full tilt)."},{"t":"Water Heat Exchanger","u":"items-cooling.html#water_heat_exchanger","k":"Cooling","d":"water_heat_exchanger Heat sink for a coolant loop that dumps heat into a body of water: place it touching water and it takes 3 kW per water source block within four blocks, up to 120 kW. Sea water works, and the water is never used up. Draws 0.5 kW."},{"t":"Coolant Distribution Unit","u":"items-cooling.html#cdu","k":"Cooling","d":"cdu Delivers liquid cooling directly to hardware. A Quantum Core only runs in a rack that has a CDU directly beside it. Draws 0.5 kW and sits on the coolant loop like a pipe."},{"t":"Exhaust Fan","u":"items-cooling.html#exhaust_fan","k":"Cooling","d":"exhaust_fan Pulls hot air out of the block in front of it and dumps it outside: up to 10 kW, only while that air is at least 1 C above ambient, so it idles (and stays clean) when there's nothing to move. Point it into a hot aisle. Moving heat outside makes smog in proportion to the heat moved; smog spreads to n"},{"t":"Raised Floor Tile","u":"items-cooling.html#raised_floor_tile","k":"Cooling","d":"raised_floor_tile A perforated floor panel that blocks airflow. Use it to seal the floor between hot and cold aisles so rack exhaust cannot creep back into the intakes."},{"t":"Thermal Scanner","u":"items-cooling.html#thermal_scanner","k":"Cooling","d":"thermal_scanner Hold it to see coloured heat particles in every warm air cell within 24 blocks: blue is cool and red is past 40 C. Right-click a rack while holding it to read the inlet and exhaust temperatures."},{"t":"Smog Scrubber","u":"items-cooling.html#smog_scrubber","k":"Cooling","d":"smog_scrubber Pulls smog back out of the air: at full power it removes 1.5 smog per second from its own chunk and 0.75 from each neighbouring chunk. It draws 6 kW, so it is cheaper than the poison. Place it near your exhaust fans."},{"t":"Respirator","u":"items-cooling.html#respirator","k":"Cooling","d":"respirator A half-face mask with charcoal filters, worn in the helmet slot. While you wear it smog can't touch you: no dizziness, blindness, cough or poison. The filter clogs in smog (about 40 minutes of it, faster when it's choking); repair it with charcoal on an anvil."},{"t":"Carbon Offset Certificate","u":"items-cooling.html#carbon_offset","k":"Cooling","d":"carbon_offset Right-click to retire it. It removes exactly one point of smog where you stand, and an unknowable amount of guilt. Comes in packs of four, because one would look bad."},{"t":"Fiber Cable","u":"items-operations.html#fiber_cable","k":"Network & Operations","d":"fiber_cable Connects racks to routers, controllers and monitoring walls. Racks only earn credits over fiber that reaches a router."},{"t":"Uplink Router","u":"items-operations.html#uplink_router","k":"Network & Operations","d":"uplink_router Provides 100 units of bandwidth to its fiber network. A rack needs bandwidth equal to its mining rate in RC/s; with less, earnings are scaled down proportionally."},{"t":"Core Router","u":"items-operations.html#core_router","k":"Network & Operations","d":"core_router A backbone router that provides 1,000 units of bandwidth, enough for a full hall of GPU blades and quantum cores."},{"t":"Photonic Spine Router","u":"items-operations.html#spine_router","k":"Network & Operations","d":"spine_router The backbone for Exascale halls: 16,000 units of bandwidth, sixteen Core Routers' worth, so one spine feeds four Exascale columns. Its light-pipe spine pulses violet while it carries traffic. Unlike lesser routers it needs power: 40 kW, and it carries nothing without it. It can't be crafted or bough"},{"t":"Monitoring Wall","u":"items-operations.html#monitoring_wall","k":"Network & Operations","d":"monitoring_wall A wall display for the fiber network. Open it to see facility power, the hottest inlet and total load."},{"t":"Storage Array","u":"items-storage.html#storage_array","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"storage_array Eight bays for Storage Drives. Like a rack it needs power (0.4 kW plus 0.15 kW per drive), takes cool air in the front and exhausts heat out the back. Link it to a Storage Terminal with Fiber Cable. If it loses power or reaches 40 C, its drives go offline until fixed; nothing is lost."},{"t":"1K Storage Drive","u":"items-storage.html#drive_1k","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"drive_1k Hot storage for 1,024 items of any mix of types. Goes in a Storage Array. Its contents stay with the drive, so you can move it to another array."},{"t":"4K Storage Drive","u":"items-storage.html#drive_4k","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"drive_4k Hot storage for 4,096 items."},{"t":"16K Storage Drive","u":"items-storage.html#drive_16k","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"drive_16k Hot storage for 16,384 items."},{"t":"64K Storage Drive","u":"items-storage.html#drive_64k","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"drive_64k Hot storage for 65,536 items."},{"t":"256K Storage Drive","u":"items-storage.html#drive_256k","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"drive_256k Hot storage for 262,144 items. Made on the Assembly Line from a 64K drive, eight RAM modules and two circuit boards (Shingled Recording), and the drive keeps everything already stored on it through the upgrade."},{"t":"1M Storage Drive","u":"items-storage.html#drive_1m","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"drive_1m Hot storage for 1,048,576 items, written into a crystal with light. Made on the Assembly Line from a 256K drive, two Photonic Chips and two HBM Stacks (Holographic Storage), contents and all."},{"t":"Tape Library","u":"items-storage.html#tape_library","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"tape_library Cold storage. Holds four Tape Cartridges of a million items each and draws only 0.3 kW. Items on tape take two seconds to mount before they reach your inventory. When your drives pass 85% full, the items you have not used for longest are archived to tape automatically."},{"t":"Tape Cartridge","u":"items-storage.html#tape_cartridge","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"tape_cartridge Cold storage for 1,048,576 items. Goes in a Tape Library. Cheap and huge, but players wait two seconds for each read."},{"t":"Storage Terminal","u":"items-storage.html#storage_terminal","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"storage_terminal Your window into storage on the same fiber network. Search, take and deposit items; craft on the 3x3 grid, which refills itself from storage; encode Recipe Patterns; and start autocrafting jobs. Ctrl-click a craftable item (marked +) to choose how many to make."},{"t":"Item Pipe","u":"items-storage.html#item_pipe","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"item_pipe Moves items between storage and the machines that use them. Connect Storage Arrays or Tape Libraries to Kids' Art Tables, Scriptorium Desks, Diesel Generators and Modular Reactors: once a second they are topped up from storage (paper, crayons, ink, fuel, Fuel Cells) and finished Art Aggregates and T"},{"t":"Storage Link","u":"items-storage.html#storage_link","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"storage_link Joins storage without another cable. Every powered Storage Link in a dimension is linked to every other, and all the storage beside them becomes one network: put a link against (or on the fiber of) each Storage Array cluster, and every terminal, Wireless Terminal, Item Pipe and machine reaching any "},{"t":"Exchange Auto-Buyer","u":"items-storage.html#auto_buyer","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"auto_buyer The company credit card, in a box. Put it on a storage network (touching a Storage Array, a Storage Link, an Item Pipe or a machine that restocks from storage) and power it, and whenever a machine on that storage restocks and storage comes up short, it buys the difference at Crypto Exchange prices o"},{"t":"Blank Pattern","u":"items-storage.html#blank_pattern","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"blank_pattern Put one in the Storage Terminal's pattern slot, lay out a recipe on the crafting grid, and press Encode to make a Recipe Pattern."},{"t":"Recipe Pattern","u":"items-storage.html#recipe_pattern","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"recipe_pattern An encoded crafting recipe. Store it anywhere in the network and its output becomes autocraftable; the planner chains patterns together for multi-step recipes."},{"t":"Wireless Transmitter","u":"items-storage.html#wireless_transmitter","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"wireless_transmitter Lets a Wireless Terminal reach this fiber network. Range starts at 16 blocks. Upgrade it in its screen with RackCoin or with resources: up to 1,024 blocks, then unlimited in this dimension, then every dimension. Each level doubles its power draw."},{"t":"Wireless Terminal","u":"items-storage.html#wireless_terminal","k":"Storage & Autocrafting","d":"wireless_terminal A Storage Terminal in your pocket. Sneak-right-click a Wireless Transmitter to link it, then right-click anywhere in range. Beyond its range or in another dimension it needs an upgraded transmitter."},{"t":"Operations Terminal","u":"items-ai.html#operations_terminal","k":"AI Contracts","d":"operations_terminal Runs the whole facility from one screen: the AI contract board (accept, pick a model, generate, deliver), what every cluster of racks is doing and its policy, the model lineup and each model's training, RackCoin income, and every problem with racks, storage, pumps, cables, generators and smog in thi"},{"t":"Kids' Art Table","u":"items-ai.html#art_table","k":"AI Contracts","d":"art_table Training data for the image model. Stock it with paper and Crayons and baby villagers come running to draw. Each kid finishes a drawing every 20 seconds (up to four kids); four drawings make a Crayon Art Aggregate. Kids at a stocked table never seem to grow up. Connect it to a Storage Array with Ite"},{"t":"Crayons","u":"items-ai.html#crayons","k":"AI Contracts","d":"crayons A box of wax crayons for the Kids' Art Table. Lasts 64 drawings."},{"t":"Crayon Art Aggregate","u":"items-ai.html#art_aggregate","k":"AI Contracts","d":"art_aggregate Four children's drawings, scanned and tagged. Upload it to one of the image models (SketchDiffusion, Nano Melon, Nano Melon Pro) at an Operations Terminal."},{"t":"Scriptorium Desk","u":"items-ai.html#writing_desk","k":"AI Contracts","d":"writing_desk Training data for the language model. Use Shackles on a librarian within six blocks to chain them to the desk, then stock it with paper and ink sacs. The librarian writes a Librarian Text Corpus every 30 seconds from two paper; an ink sac lasts four. Connect it to a Storage Array with Item Pipe and "},{"t":"Shackles","u":"items-ai.html#shackles","k":"AI Contracts","d":"shackles Use on a librarian near a free Scriptorium Desk to chain them to it. You can see the chain from their wrists to the desk. Only librarians can write."},{"t":"Librarian Text Corpus","u":"items-ai.html#text_corpus","k":"AI Contracts","d":"text_corpus Several thousand words a librarian really didn't want to write. Upload it to one of the Gemerald language models (Flash-Lite, Flash, Pro) at an Operations Terminal."},{"t":"Generated Image","u":"items-ai.html#generated_image","k":"AI Contracts","d":"generated_image An AI-generated image, made for a contract. It remembers its prompt and quality: keep spares, because clients often ask for the same thing again."},{"t":"Generated Document","u":"items-ai.html#generated_document","k":"AI Contracts","d":"generated_document AI-generated writing: essays, homework, legal documents and worse. It remembers its prompt and quality, so a spare can fill a repeat order."},{"t":"Uranium Ore","u":"items-nuclear.html#uranium_ore","k":"Nuclear","d":"uranium_ore Faintly glowing ore found deep down, from Y -64 to Y 16, in small veins. Mine it with an iron pickaxe for Raw Uranium (Fortune helps, Silk Touch keeps the ore). Raw Uranium can't be smelted: it goes to a Uranium Mill, two to a Yellowcake, and a Fuel Cell takes twelve."},{"t":"Raw Uranium","u":"items-nuclear.html#raw_uranium","k":"Nuclear","d":"raw_uranium Uranium ore straight out of the ground. Feed it to a Uranium Mill to make Yellowcake."},{"t":"Uranium Mill","u":"items-nuclear.html#uranium_mill","k":"Nuclear","d":"uranium_mill Grinds and leaches Raw Uranium into Yellowcake: two ore make one. Like every nuclear machine it is a multiblock: build Uranium Mills into a solid cube, 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research), to make it work at all. Every block in the cube is a core: each core finishes one batch every 30 seco"},{"t":"Yellowcake","u":"items-nuclear.html#yellowcake","k":"Nuclear","d":"yellowcake Uranium oxide concentrate from a Uranium Mill. Six of it go into a Gas Centrifuge for one Enriched Uranium."},{"t":"Gas Centrifuge","u":"items-nuclear.html#gas_centrifuge","k":"Nuclear","d":"gas_centrifuge Enriches uranium: spins six Yellowcake into one Enriched Uranium and four Depleted Uranium, two minutes per batch per core, 60 kW per working core. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research) Gas Centrifuges (a real cascade, and a real power bill)."},{"t":"Enriched Uranium","u":"items-nuclear.html#enriched_uranium","k":"Nuclear","d":"enriched_uranium Reactor-grade uranium from a Gas Centrifuge. A Fuel Fabricator makes a Fuel Cell from one, with two Steel Ingots."},{"t":"Depleted Uranium","u":"items-nuclear.html#depleted_uranium","k":"Nuclear","d":"depleted_uranium What the Gas Centrifuge leaves behind: dense, heavy and good at stopping radiation. A Cask Sealer uses it to shield Spent Fuel."},{"t":"Fuel Fabricator","u":"items-nuclear.html#fuel_fabricator","k":"Nuclear","d":"fuel_fabricator The only way to make Fuel Cells: one Enriched Uranium and two Steel Ingots make one cell, a minute per batch per core, 30 kW per working core. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research)."},{"t":"Fuel Cell","u":"items-nuclear.html#fuel_cell","k":"Nuclear","d":"fuel_cell The fuel for the Modular Reactor: one cell runs a reactor for 30 minutes at full output and leaves a Spent Fuel rod behind. It can't be crafted or bought: a Fuel Fabricator makes it from Enriched Uranium. Item Pipes can deliver them from storage."},{"t":"Modular Reactor","u":"items-nuclear.html#modular_reactor","k":"Nuclear","d":"modular_reactor A small modular reactor: 500 kW of steady power, burning one Fuel Cell every 30 minutes at full output (less at part load), and every spent cell comes out as Spent Fuel in its waste slot. Build reactors into a solid cube, 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research), and they form one reactor array"},{"t":"Spent Fuel","u":"items-nuclear.html#spent_fuel","k":"Nuclear","d":"spent_fuel A burnt-out Fuel Cell, and very much still radioactive. Carrying it gives you Radiation Sickness, worse the more you carry. Pipe it out of the reactor into storage and on to a Cask Sealer, which seals four of them into a Waste Cask."},{"t":"Cask Sealer","u":"items-nuclear.html#cask_sealer","k":"Nuclear","d":"cask_sealer Seals nuclear waste away for good: four Spent Fuel and four Depleted Uranium (the shielding) make one Sealed Waste Cask, a minute per batch per core, 15 kW per working core. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research)."},{"t":"Sealed Waste Cask","u":"items-nuclear.html#waste_cask","k":"Nuclear","d":"waste_cask Four Spent Fuel rods sealed behind steel and depleted uranium. Completely safe to carry, stack and store: line them up on a concrete pad somewhere and try not to think about the next ten thousand years."},{"t":"Wafer Fab","u":"items-rnd.html#wafer_fab","k":"R&D and the Frontier","d":"wafer_fab A chip fab in a box, or rather in a cube: it etches sixteen Silicon and four GPU Chips into one Wafer-Scale Engine, five minutes per batch per core, 400 kW per working core. It is a multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research) Wafer Fabs. It does nothing until the "},{"t":"Wafer-Scale Engine","u":"items-rnd.html#wafer_scale_engine","k":"R&D and the Frontier","d":"wafer_scale_engine The endgame AI module: a whole silicon wafer as one chip. It lends 150 AI compute and 40 general compute (three Quantum Cores' worth of AI) and draws 2 to 16 kW, so a full rack is 128 kW of heat. It can't mine, it is liquid-cooled, and it boots in 20 seconds. It can't be crafted or bought: only a Wa"},{"t":"Silicon Foundry","u":"items-rnd.html#silicon_foundry","k":"R&D and the Frontier","d":"silicon_foundry Melts quartz and sand into silicon by the bucketload: two Nether Quartz and four Sand make eight Silicon, twenty seconds per batch per core, 40 kW per working core. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research). Handy for feeding a Wafer Fab, which eats sixteen Si"},{"t":"E-Waste Recycler","u":"items-rnd.html#ewaste_recycler","k":"R&D and the Frontier","d":"ewaste_recycler Shreds dead hardware back into parts: each Failed Module gives three Silicon and a Copper Wire, fifteen seconds per batch per core, 10 kW per working core. Fried Modules (stripped from flare-fried racks) pay out by tier, up to 16 Silicon and 3 GPU Chips. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up "},{"t":"HEROBRINE-1 Weights","u":"items-rnd.html#agi_weights","k":"R&D and the Frontier","d":"agi_weights A thumb drive holding the weights of the AGI you trained. It left a copy in your outbox, presumably as a gesture of goodwill. It does nothing. It is watching. Do not open-source it."},{"t":"CVD Furnace","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#cvd_furnace","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"cvd_furnace Grows Graphene Sheets by chemical vapour deposition: four Coke and a Hydrogen Canister make two sheets, a minute per batch per core, 150 kW per working core. A multiblock like the other processing cubes (2x2x2 and up), fed and emptied by hand, hopper or Item Pipe; on a pipe to a Hydrogen Tank it dra"},{"t":"Graphene Sheet","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#graphene_sheet","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"graphene_sheet Carbon one atom thick: stronger than steel, better at heat than copper. Only a CVD Furnace makes it, from Coke and hydrogen. Goes into FPGAs, HBM Stacks, Neuromorphic Cores, wafer dicing and Liquid-Immersion Racks. Can't be bought."},{"t":"Epitaxy Reactor","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#epitaxy_reactor","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"epitaxy_reactor Recovers the trace of gallium in bauxite and grows it into crystal: eight Raw Bauxite and a Hydrogen Canister make one Gallium Nitride, 90 seconds per batch per core, 250 kW per working core. A processing cube (2x2x2 and up), locked until Advanced Materials is researched."},{"t":"Gallium Nitride","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#gallium_nitride","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"gallium_nitride A blue-violet crystal grown in an Epitaxy Reactor from bauxite's gallium. The base of a Photonic Interconnect. Can't be bought."},{"t":"Superconducting Wire","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#superconducting_wire","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"superconducting_wire Wire with no resistance at all, as long as it stays very cold. Built on the Assembly Line once Advanced Materials is researched: a Cryo Coil wound with eight Copper Wire, two Hydrogen Canisters of coolant, then welded and riveted. Used in Cryostats, Quantum Annealers and Exascale Cabinets."},{"t":"Chiplet (Bronze Bin)","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#chiplet_bronze","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"chiplet_bronze A die cut from a wafer that tested slow. Once Chiplets and Advanced Packaging is researched, an Assembly Line dices a Wafer-Scale Engine (weld, two Graphene Sheets, rivet) into eight chiplets, and the whole wafer bins as one: 60% bronze, 30% silver, 10% gold. Bronze goes into HBM Stacks and NPU Infe"},{"t":"Chiplet (Silver Bin)","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#chiplet_silver","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"chiplet_silver A die that tested well: 30% of diced wafers bin silver. Neuromorphic Cores and Photonic Interconnects need silver. Craft one into a bronze chiplet if you need bronze more."},{"t":"Chiplet (Gold Bin)","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#chiplet_gold","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"chiplet_gold The silicon lottery's jackpot: only one wafer in ten bins gold. The Photonic Tensor Core needs two and the Quantum Annealer one. Craft it into a silver chiplet if you must, but you'll regret it."},{"t":"HBM Memory Stack","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#hbm_stack","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"hbm_stack Four memory dies stacked on a chiplet and bonded with graphene. Built on the Assembly Line from a RAM Module: three more RAM Modules, a bronze Chiplet and a Graphene Sheet, then riveted. Every tier 3 and 4 module and the Exascale Cabinet use them."},{"t":"Photonic Interconnect","u":"items-advanced_chips.html#photonic_chip","k":"Advanced Materials and Chips","d":"photonic_chip Moves data as light. Built on the Assembly Line from Gallium Nitride once Silicon Photonics is researched: a silver Chiplet, four Fiber Cable, welded and riveted. Four go in a Photonic Tensor Core and eight in an Exascale Cabinet."},{"t":"High-Density Rack","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#high_density_rack","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"high_density_rack Tier 2: twelve module bays and a radiator door built into its back. On a coolant loop, the door catches up to 40 kW of the rack's hot air into the loop before it reaches the room, on top of the 85% that liquid-cooled modules send down the pipe; off a loop it is just a bigger rack. Its fans draw 0.5 "},{"t":"Liquid-Immersion Rack","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#immersion_rack","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"immersion_rack Tier 3: sixteen bays, with every module sunk in dielectric fluid. All of its heat goes into the coolant loop and none into the air, so a hall of them needs no CRACs or fans at all, only sinks. It won't run off a loop, even with air-cooled modules in it, and its pumps draw 2 kW. It's also the space r"},{"t":"Exascale Cabinet","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#exascale_cabinet","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"exascale_cabinet Tier 4: twenty-four bays on a photonic fabric. Everything in it mines and lends compute 20% faster, its mining needs only half the fiber bandwidth, and like an immersion rack all its heat goes to the loop. It only takes tier 2 hardware and up (no Pi Nodes, 1U Servers, ASIC Miners or Crafting Coproce"},{"t":"Blade Chassis","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#blade_chassis","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"blade_chassis An empty module sled: the base for every new module made on the Assembly Line. The first part an Assembly Robot fits decides what it becomes: GPU Chips make an FPGA Module, bronze Chiplets an NPU, silver Chiplets a Neuromorphic Core, gold Chiplets a Photonic Tensor Core, and Superconducting Wire a Q"},{"t":"FPGA Module","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#fpga_module","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"fpga_module Reprogrammable hardware: right-click it to reflash it for mining (8 RC/s), AI (8 AI compute) or autocrafting (20 general compute), about two thirds of what the specialist module does in each. Draws 0.4 to 2.5 kW and is air-cooled. Take it out of the rack to reflash it. Built on the Assembly Line fro"},{"t":"NPU Inference Card","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#npu_card","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"npu_card An inference accelerator: 32 AI compute on 0.3 to 1.8 kW, air-cooled, but only for contract generation and Compute Leases. It never helps train models, R&D or frontier runs. It can't mine. Built on the Assembly Line from a Blade Chassis (four bronze Chiplets, an HBM Stack, two Circuit Boards, rivet)"},{"t":"Neuromorphic Core","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#neuromorphic_core","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"neuromorphic_core Spiking silicon modelled on a brain: 80 general compute for autocrafting and general research on just 0.2 to 1.2 kW, air-cooled. It can't mine and lends little AI. Built on the Assembly Line from a Blade Chassis (two silver Chiplets, an HBM Stack, two Graphene Sheets, weld, rivet) once Chiplets and "},{"t":"Photonic Tensor Core","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#photonic_tensor_core","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"photonic_tensor_core Matrix maths done in light: 300 AI compute and 60 general compute in one bay, twice a Wafer-Scale Engine, on 3 to 12 kW. Liquid-cooled; it can't mine. Built on the Assembly Line from a Blade Chassis (two gold Chiplets, four Photonic Interconnects, two HBM Stacks, weld, rivet) once Silicon Photonics "},{"t":"Quantum Annealer","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#quantum_annealer","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"quantum_annealer The best miner there is: 120 RC/s, twice a Quantum Core, on 4 to 14 kW, liquid-cooled. Its rack only runs with a cold Cryostat directly beside it, and each running Annealer boils off a Hydrogen Canister every 5 minutes. It boots in 25 seconds. Built on the Assembly Line from a Blade Chassis (four Su"},{"t":"Topological Core","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#topological_core","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"topological_core The best general-purpose module there is: 100 RC/s of mining, 120 general compute (more than a Neuromorphic Core) and 120 AI compute in one bay, on 5 to 20 kW, liquid-cooled. Its qubits are braided anyons, so they hardly decohere, but like a Quantum Core its rack only runs with a CDU directly beside"},{"t":"Cryostat","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#cryostat","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"cryostat Keeps Quantum Annealers a whisker above absolute zero. A rack holding Annealers only runs with a cold Cryostat directly beside it: one with power (15 kW) and Hydrogen Canisters in its slot. The liquid hydrogen boils off as it works, one canister every 5 minutes for each running Annealer beside it (a"},{"t":"Pod Port","u":"items-advanced_racks.html#pod_port","k":"Advanced Racks and Modules","d":"pod_port Turns a solid block of High-Density Racks or better into a Compute Pod. Build a solid cuboid of at least four racks (no more than 2 x 4 x 8, and 64 in all), touch it with a powered Pod Port, and put Photonic Interconnects in its slot, one for every four racks: the fabric then lends 10% more AI compu"},{"t":"Grid-Tie Substation","u":"items-utilities.html#grid_substation","k":"Utility Plants","d":"grid_substation Sells your spare power to the outside grid for RackCoin. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research) on your power network. Each core exports up to 1 MW, but only what solar panels, wind turbines and reactors have left over once everything else is powered and th"},{"t":"Heat Recovery Plant","u":"items-utilities.html#heat_recovery_plant","k":"Utility Plants","d":"heat_recovery_plant District heating: a heat sink on your coolant loop that sells the heat to the villages around it. A multiblock: build a solid cube of 2x2x2 up to 5x5x5 (or 10x10x10 with research) and pipe it into a loop. Every villager within 64 blocks takes 50 kW (half again in cold biomes, less in hot ones), up t"},{"t":"Electrolyser","u":"items-industry.html#electrolyser","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"electrolyser Splits water into hydrogen with an absurd amount of electricity: each core rolls an Aluminum Ingot into a tank and fills it, one Hydrogen Canister every 30 seconds, drawing 4,000 kW while it works. With a Hydrogen Tank touching it (or on the same Item Pipe or Storage Link network) it fills the tank "},{"t":"Hydrogen Canister","u":"items-industry.html#hydrogen_canister","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"hydrogen_canister An aluminum tank of compressed hydrogen from an Electrolyser: about 120 MJ of electricity went into it. A Drone Dock burns one every eight trips, an Assembly Robot can fit one into a drone as its fuel tank, and a launch pad will want them by the thousand."},{"t":"Hydrogen Tank","u":"items-industry.html#hydrogen_tank","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"hydrogen_tank Holds hydrogen as gas, once Cryogenic Hydrogen Storage is researched. Each block holds 256 canisters' worth, and a solid cube (2x2x2 up to 5x5x5, or 10x10x10 with research) holds more per block: 40% more in a 5x5x5. An Electrolyser touching a tank, or on the same Item Pipe or Storage Link network, p"},{"t":"Tanker Drone","u":"items-industry.html#tanker_drone","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"tanker_drone A big, fast hydrogen hauler, and the one drone you can't build: it is only sold at the Crypto Exchange, for a great deal of RackCoin, and only once Cryogenic Hydrogen Storage is researched. Put it in a Hydrogen Tank's slot and it flies a stack of canisters at a time from the tank to any Drone Dock, "},{"t":"Conveyor Belt","u":"items-industry.html#conveyor_belt","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"conveyor_belt Carries one item at a time along the way it faces, a block a second. Drop an item on it, right-click it with one, or feed it with a hopper; right-click with an empty hand to take the item back. At the far end the item moves onto the next belt (which can turn), into a chest, hopper or any container, "},{"t":"Belt Loader","u":"items-industry.html#belt_loader","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"belt_loader Feeds an Assembly Line straight from storage. Put it on an Item Pipe that reaches a Storage Array, face it at the first Conveyor Belt, and put one of the item you want in its slot (that one is only a sample and stays put). Whenever the belt in front is empty it takes another from storage and puts it"},{"t":"Belt Unloader","u":"items-industry.html#belt_unloader","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"belt_unloader Files whatever comes off the end of an Assembly Line into storage. Point the last Conveyor Belt into it and put it on an Item Pipe that reaches a Storage Array: finished drones, arrays, nacelles and the rest land in its nine-slot buffer and go into storage once a second. Hoppers can empty it too. Ne"},{"t":"Storage Exporter","u":"items-industry.html#storage_exporter","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"storage_exporter Sends items from storage into whatever it faces. Put it on an Item Pipe that reaches a Storage Array, face it at a machine or container (a Site Planner, a Launch Control, a chest), and put samples of what to send in its nine slots: once a second it tops the block in front up to a stack of each, thro"},{"t":"Welding Robot","u":"items-industry.html#welding_arm","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"welding_arm A robot arm with a plasma torch. Place it beside a Conveyor Belt, facing the belt, and it welds every workpiece that needs welding as it passes: six seconds per weld, drawing 1,500 kW while the torch is lit and 1 kW waiting. Below full power it welds slower, and under 10% it stops. Sparks fly; don't"},{"t":"Riveting Robot","u":"items-industry.html#riveting_arm","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"riveting_arm A robot arm with a pneumatic rivet gun. Place it beside a Conveyor Belt, facing the belt, and it rivets every workpiece that needs riveting as it passes: four seconds a job, drawing 600 kW while it hammers and 1 kW waiting. Loud."},{"t":"Assembly Robot","u":"items-industry.html#assembly_arm","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"assembly_arm A robot arm with a gripper and nine slots of parts. Place it beside a Conveyor Belt, facing the belt, and stock it with what the line needs (Electric Motors, Circuit Boards, Hydrogen Canisters): it installs parts into every workpiece whose next step it can do, three seconds per step at 250 kW, and k"},{"t":"Electric Motor","u":"items-industry.html#electric_motor","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"electric_motor Copper windings around an iron core in a steel can. Conveyor Belts and robot arms run on them, and a Maintenance Drone needs four for its rotors."},{"t":"Drone Frame","u":"items-industry.html#drone_frame","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"drone_frame The bare airframe of a Maintenance Drone. Put it on an Assembly Line: an Assembly Robot fits four Electric Motors, a Welding Robot welds the mounts, Assembly Robots fit two Circuit Boards and a Hydrogen Canister, and a Riveting Robot closes the shell. A frame that falls off the line half-built remem"},{"t":"Maintenance Drone","u":"items-industry.html#maintenance_drone","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"maintenance_drone A quadcopter with a gripper and a strong sense of duty. Load it into a Drone Dock and it flies out to fix things on its own: failed rack modules, cut cables and tripped breakers. It is built on an Assembly Line, not a crafting table."},{"t":"Drone Dock","u":"items-industry.html#drone_dock","k":"Industry and Drones","d":"drone_dock Home base for Maintenance Drones, and the end of walking to every broken thing in a big hall. Load drones into its first slot (up to eight), Hydrogen Canisters into the second, and spares into the rest: rack modules and Repair Kits. Better, put it on an Item Pipe that reaches storage: it then takes "},{"t":"Solar Array Frame","u":"items-renewables.html#solar_array_frame","k":"Wind and Solar at Scale","d":"solar_array_frame The bare 3x2 frame of a Solar Array. On an Assembly Line it gets 6 Solar Panels, 2 Circuit Boards, a weld and rivets."},{"t":"Solar Array","u":"items-renewables.html#solar_array","k":"Wind and Solar at Scale","d":"solar_array Six panels' worth of solar on one 3x2 frame, built on the Assembly Line. Place it like a bed: it takes three blocks to your right and two ahead, and needs them clear. In full sun it makes 150 kW (smog dims it, night stops it) and every array touching another shares its power, so a field of them need"},{"t":"Tower Frame","u":"items-renewables.html#tower_frame","k":"Wind and Solar at Scale","d":"tower_frame A ring of steel for a Wind Tower. A Welding Robot and a Riveting Robot turn it into a Tower Section."},{"t":"Tower Section","u":"items-renewables.html#tower_section","k":"Wind and Solar at Scale","d":"tower_section One more block of Wind Tower, built on the Assembly Line from a Tower Frame (a weld and some rivets). Stack them straight up and put a Wind Tower Nacelle on top; power runs down the tower to the ground. Thin enough that air passes it."},{"t":"Nacelle Frame","u":"items-renewables.html#nacelle_frame","k":"Wind and Solar at Scale","d":"nacelle_frame The empty shell of a Wind Tower Nacelle. On an Assembly Line it gets 8 Electric Motors (the generator), 4 Circuit Boards, a weld, 16 Copper Wire and rivets."},{"t":"Wind Tower Nacelle","u":"items-renewables.html#wind_nacelle","k":"Wind and Solar at Scale","d":"wind_nacelle The business end of a Wind Tower: a generator with three great blades, built on the Assembly Line. Put it on top of at least ten Tower Sections stacked in a column, facing into open air: the blades need a clear 5x5 in front of it. It makes 600 kW at Y 130, more higher up (up to 750 kW above Y 150), "},{"t":"Survey Stake","u":"items-construction.html#survey_stake","k":"Site Construction","d":"survey_stake A stick with a flag on it, for telling a Site Planner where to build. Use it on the ground at one corner of the site and then at the opposite corner; while you hold it the site is outlined. Use it on a Site Planner to hand the site over. Sneak-use to start again."},{"t":"Site Planner","u":"items-construction.html#site_planner","k":"Site Construction","d":"site_planner Builds whole power plants while you do something else. Mark a site with a Survey Stake (up to 48 x 48, within 96 blocks), use the stake on the planner, pick a layout (Solar Field, Wind Farm, Data Hall or Reactor Cube) and press Start. Construction Drones clear the site first: grass, flowers, snow, l"},{"t":"Procurement Wall","u":"items-construction.html#procurement_wall","k":"Site Construction","d":"procurement_wall A Monitoring Wall that has been to business school. Plug it into the same power grid as your Site Planners and it keeps their books: every purchase their drones make at the Crypto Exchange, itemised (what, how many, what each cost, what part of the job it was for, how long ago), with running totals "},{"t":"Heavy Drone Frame","u":"items-construction.html#heavy_drone_frame","k":"Site Construction","d":"heavy_drone_frame A bigger airframe for drones that lift things. What it becomes depends on the first part an Assembly Robot fits: two Sticky Pistons make a Construction Drone, a Diamond Shovel a Terraforming Drone. Either way it then gets four Electric Motors, two Circuit Boards, a weld and rivets."},{"t":"Construction Drone","u":"items-construction.html#construction_drone","k":"Site Construction","d":"construction_drone A heavy-lift quadcopter in hazard stripes. Load it into a Site Planner and it clears sites, places Solar Arrays and Wind Towers, and lays cable, carrying its materials out from storage and bringing back whatever it cleared. Built on the Assembly Line from a Heavy Drone Frame."},{"t":"Terraforming Drone","u":"items-construction.html#terraforming_drone","k":"Site Construction","d":"terraforming_drone A quadcopter with a scoop. Load it into a Site Planner and it levels the site: it digs up to five blocks off the high spots each trip and drops them into the low ones, takes spare earth home to storage, and brings dirt or cobblestone from storage when there is more hole than hill. When the ground is"},{"t":"Column Skid Frame","u":"items-construction.html#skid_frame","k":"Site Construction","d":"skid_frame A steel pallet with corner posts, for packing a whole Data Hall column. Put it on an Assembly Line: the racks an Assembly Robot fits first decide the skid (Server Racks, High-Density Racks, Immersion Racks or Exascale Cabinets), then come two CDUs, a weld, the Chillers and Core Routers, and rivets."},{"t":"Server Rack Column Skid","u":"items-construction.html#server_rack_skid","k":"Site Construction","d":"server_rack_skid A Data Hall column packed on a pallet: four Server Racks two high, two CDUs between them, Chillers over the CDUs and Core Routers over the racks (its tooltip says how many), all from your own parts on the Assembly Line, so nothing is bought and no drone flies. Use it on the ground and it unfolds awa"},{"t":"High-Density Column Skid","u":"items-construction.html#high_density_rack_skid","k":"Site Construction","d":"high_density_rack_skid A Data Hall column packed on a pallet: four High-Density Racks two high, two CDUs between them, Chillers over the CDUs and Core Routers over the racks (its tooltip says how many), all from your own parts on the Assembly Line, so nothing is bought and no drone flies. Use it on the ground and it unfol"},{"t":"Immersion Column Skid","u":"items-construction.html#immersion_rack_skid","k":"Site Construction","d":"immersion_rack_skid A Data Hall column packed on a pallet: four Immersion Racks two high, two CDUs between them, Chillers over the CDUs and Core Routers over the racks (its tooltip says how many), all from your own parts on the Assembly Line, so nothing is bought and no drone flies. Use it on the ground and it unfolds "},{"t":"Exascale Column Skid","u":"items-construction.html#exascale_cabinet_skid","k":"Site Construction","d":"exascale_cabinet_skid A Data Hall column packed on a pallet: four Exascale Cabinets two high, two CDUs between them, Chillers over the CDUs and Core Routers over the racks (its tooltip says how many), all from your own parts on the Assembly Line, so nothing is bought and no drone flies. Use it on the ground and it unfold"},{"t":"Vehicle Chassis","u":"items-construction.html#vehicle_chassis","k":"Site Construction","d":"vehicle_chassis A welding jig on a pallet, for building a Superb Warfare vehicle on an Assembly Line. Right-click the air to pick which vehicle (sneak to go back), then put it on a Conveyor Belt and it sits on the line as a see-through outline of the real thing. Robots beside the belt, facing it from up to three bl"},{"t":"Blueprint","u":"items-wiring.html#blueprint","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"blueprint A sheet of the future. Blank until a Blueprint Scanner writes a box of Rackcraft blocks into it: their kinds, which way they face, and for a rack the module that fills most of its bays. Put a written one in a Site Planner's material slots and pick the Blueprint layout, and the drones print it again "},{"t":"Blueprint Scanner","u":"items-wiring.html#blueprint_scanner","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"blueprint_scanner Use on a block for one corner and another for the opposite; the box is outlined while you hold it. Use in the air to scan it into a blank Blueprint in your inventory, up to 64 blocks a side. It only reads Rackcraft blocks (the printer's drones carry nothing else), and nothing inside machines but a r"},{"t":"Trunk Bundle","u":"items-wiring.html#trunk_bundle","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"trunk_bundle One thick conduit that carries power, coolant and fiber at the same time: three networks in one block, instead of three runs side by side. It joins any power cable, coolant pipe or fiber cable it touches, and machines of those kinds, each on its own lane. A cut trunk cuts all three lanes at once, an"},{"t":"Cable Planner","u":"items-wiring.html#cable_planner","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"cable_planner Draw a cable run before you lay it. Right-click a block face to set the start, another for the end: a ghost of the route hangs in the world, red where something is in the way. Right-click in the air to cycle the route style (Straight, Zig-zag, Shortest, Hug the Walls) and sneak-right-click in the ai"},{"t":"Patch Panel","u":"items-wiring.html#patch_panel","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"patch_panel Rewire without touching a cable. Each of its six faces has a port number from 0 to 8: cables and machines on two faces with the same port (not 0) are joined, on every network at once; a face on port 0 is isolated. Right-click a face with an empty hand to cycle its port, use a Name Tag to label the p"},{"t":"Pylon","u":"items-wiring.html#pylon","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"pylon A lattice mast for long-span power. Link two Pylons up to 128 blocks apart with a Pylon Linker and they behave as one cable, strung with an overhead line that sags across the gap. Every link wastes 3% of the network's load per 64 blocks of span. Both ends must be loaded to carry power, and a mast wi"},{"t":"Superconducting Pylon","u":"items-wiring.html#superconducting_pylon","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"superconducting_pylon A Pylon wound with Superconducting Wire. Two of these link up to 256 blocks apart and waste nothing at all. A link to an ordinary Pylon falls back to the ordinary range and loss."},{"t":"Pylon Linker","u":"items-wiring.html#pylon_linker","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"pylon_linker Use it on a Pylon, then on another, to string a span between them. Sneak-use on a linked Pylon to cut its spans. Range 128 blocks, or 256 between two Superconducting Pylons."},{"t":"Power Beacon","u":"items-wiring.html#power_beacon","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"power_beacon Beams power through the air to any Beacon Receiver within 16 blocks, no cable needed. It draws what the receivers' network asks for, up to 1 MW, plus 15% lost to the sky. Receivers are shared out evenly, and a beam is a line of sparks you can follow. Needs Wireless Power."},{"t":"Beacon Receiver","u":"items-wiring.html#beacon_receiver","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"beacon_receiver The catching end of a Power Beacon: it pairs with the nearest beacon in range and feeds whatever power network it touches, drawn before any other source. Out of range, it just sits there looking smug."},{"t":"Battery Cell","u":"items-wiring.html#battery_cell","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"battery_cell A fat little power cell for hand tools. The Constructor's Gauntlet and the Terraformer Cannon burn them. Not rechargeable; it was never going to be."},{"t":"Constructor's Gauntlet","u":"items-wiring.html#constructor_gauntlet","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"constructor_gauntlet A powered glove that builds from a distance. It reaches 32 blocks, takes blocks from the storage network of a Wireless Terminal in your inventory and returns what it deletes there, and spends a Battery Cell for every 16 uses. Sneak-use in the air to cycle modes: Line, Plane, Box fill, Swap and Delet"},{"t":"Terraformer Cannon","u":"items-wiring.html#terraformer_cannon","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"terraformer_cannon Flattens, fills or hollows ground in a radius, working outward from where it hits over a moment. Right-click a block to fire at it. Sneak-use in the air to cycle the setting (Flatten, Fill, Hollow, Smooth; round or square); use it in the air to cycle the radius from 3 to 16. Every 256 blocks moved b"},{"t":"Heavy Terraformer","u":"items-wiring.html#heavy_terraformer","k":"Building and Wiring","d":"heavy_terraformer The Terraformer Cannon scaled up for airfields and pads: radius 4 to 64 in steps of 4, round or square, and four times the ground per Hydrogen Canister (1,024 blocks). Right-click a block to fire; use in the air for the radius, sneak-use in the air for the setting (Flatten, Fill, Hollow, Smooth; rou"},{"t":"Grapple","u":"items-movement.html#grapple_hook","k":"Getting Around","d":"grapple_hook Use it to fire a hook at the block you are looking at, up to 40 blocks away, and be pulled to it, faster as you go. Sneak to let go. It burns a Battery Cell a shot, and the last bit of the fall at the end is cushioned."},{"t":"Hydrogen Jetpack","u":"items-movement.html#hydrogen_jetpack","k":"Getting Around","d":"hydrogen_jetpack A chestplate with a thrust bottle. Hold jump to climb, sneak in the air to hover (half the fuel), sprint while climbing to boost. It burns a Hydrogen Canister for every thirty seconds of thrust, taken from the canisters you carry or a Wireless Terminal's storage. Run dry in a long fall and it vents "},{"t":"Mag-Lev Rail","u":"items-movement.html#maglev_rail","k":"Getting Around","d":"maglev_rail A slim rail for a Mag-Lev line; three to a craft. Rails and Stations that touch form a line a car can run along, and a rail is also a power-network block, so it carries power from any cable it touches. A cut rail breaks the line: a Repair Kit splices it. Inert until Magnetic Levitation is researched"},{"t":"Mag-Lev Station","u":"items-movement.html#maglev_station","k":"Getting Around","d":"maglev_station Where a line starts and ends. Right-click to ride to its destination (the nearest station until you choose); sneak-right-click with an empty hand to cycle destinations down the line. The car carries one rider, glides at up to 54 blocks a second, and draws 800 kW from this station's network while it "},{"t":"Quantum Teleport Pad","u":"items-movement.html#teleport_pad","k":"Getting Around","d":"teleport_pad Stand on it for two seconds and you arrive on the pad it is entangled with, anywhere in the dimension. Entangle two pads by using a Linked Shard on one and then the other (a Dimensional Shard for pads in different dimensions, at twenty times the cost); sneak-use a shard to cut a pair. Each pad needs"},{"t":"Linked Shard","u":"items-movement.html#linked_shard","k":"Getting Around","d":"linked_shard Half of an entangled pair, waiting for a pad. Use it on a Quantum Teleport Pad, then on a second one, and the two are entangled and the shard is used up. Sneak-use it on a pad to cut its pair."},{"t":"Dimensional Shard","u":"items-movement.html#dimensional_shard","k":"Getting Around","d":"dimensional_shard A Linked Shard with a Nether Star in it. It entangles pads in different dimensions, which cost twenty times the power and four times the hydrogen to jump between."},{"t":"Hydrogen Flamethrower","u":"items-defence.html#hydrogen_flamethrower","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"hydrogen_flamethrower Hold use to spray an 8-block jet of fire that droops a little like the burning fuel it is: it sets mobs alight, burns cobwebs and lights flammable plain blocks, never Rackcraft machines or chests. One Hydrogen Canister is about 20 seconds of fire. Heat builds slowly and it locks out for a few second"},{"t":"Arc Coil","u":"items-defence.html#arc_coil","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"arc_coil Click for lightning that hits a mob and chains to up to three more within 5 blocks. Zap a Rackcraft machine and every breaker on its power network trips; robots and drones take extra damage and freeze for 20 seconds. A Battery Cell is eight shots. Needs Directed Energy. Mk II (Mil-Spec Refits, on th"},{"t":"Railgun","u":"items-defence.html#railgun","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"railgun Hold to charge for up to two seconds, release to fire a slug that pierces mobs and up to three plain blocks (never a Rackcraft block, never bedrock). Damage scales with charge, the recoil is not subtle. Each shot needs a Steel Slug and a quarter of a Battery Cell; it runs very hot and has a long coo"},{"t":"Plasma Rifle","u":"items-defence.html#plasma_rifle","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"plasma_rifle Click for a bolt of plasma with splash damage and a glowing scorch. A Hydrogen Canister loads twelve bolts and a Battery Cell about thirty. It overheats in seconds unless a Coolant Pack is in your pack, in which case it keeps going and the pack slowly wears down. Needs Plasma Weapons. Mk II (Mil-Spe"},{"t":"Lance Laser","u":"items-defence.html#lance_laser","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"lance_laser Hold for a continuous beam up to 32 blocks that melts cobweb and ice (glass is safe, unless the config says otherwise), sets things alight and goes round armour. A Battery Cell is about 20 seconds of beam, and it heats up extremely fast: short bursts only. Needs Plasma Weapons. Mk II (Mil-Spec Refit"},{"t":"EMP Grenade","u":"items-defence.html#emp_grenade","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"emp_grenade Throw it and every Rackcraft machine, drone, turret and security robot within 10 blocks switches off for three minutes. PDUs trip, robots freeze mid-step, vault doors give up and slide open. Nothing is damaged, only silenced. The polite way into a Military Base. Needs Directed Energy."},{"t":"Steel Slug","u":"items-defence.html#steel_slug","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"steel_slug A hunk of steel with ambitions, ammunition for the Railgun. One steel ingot and a nugget make four."},{"t":"Coolant Pack","u":"items-defence.html#coolant_pack","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"coolant_pack A pack of cold stuff. Carry one and every energy weapon in your inventory vents ten times faster, which is the only way a Plasma Rifle keeps up. Each shot while the weapon is warm wears it down; when it is spent it is spent."},{"t":"Laser Sentry","u":"items-defence.html#laser_sentry","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"laser_sentry A power-hungry turret on a swivel that shoots visible, dodgeable bolts at hostile mobs and angry guards within 16 blocks (never players, pets, villagers or drones). It draws 1.5 kW, checks for targets twice a second and heats up as it fires: put it on a coolant loop with a tower or chiller and it sh"},{"t":"Arc Sentry","u":"items-defence.html#arc_sentry","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"arc_sentry A turret that throws lightning at the nearest hostile target and chains it to two more, and is rude to robots. Same power, coolant and targeting rules as the Laser Sentry, shorter and hotter. Needs Directed Energy."},{"t":"Railgun Sentry","u":"items-defence.html#railgun_sentry","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"railgun_sentry A slow, loud turret that fires one piercing slug every two seconds through everything between it and the target. Same power, coolant and targeting rules as the Laser Sentry. Needs Directed Energy."},{"t":"Blast Door","u":"items-defence.html#blast_door","k":"Arms and Ruins","d":"blast_door A vault door you cannot mine. It stays shut while a live Power Cable touches it and slides open the moment that cable is cut or broken, or an EMP goes off nearby. Nothing is destroyed; it just retracts, with a sulk. Found in the Military Base and in the odd hostile data center."},{"t":"Launch Pad","u":"items-space.html#launch_pad","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"launch_pad Blast-proof concrete with a flame trench. Lay nine in a flat 3x3 square with open sky above the middle, put a Launch Control beside it, and you have a spaceport. The rocket stands on the centre block."},{"t":"Launch Tower","u":"items-space.html#launch_tower","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"launch_tower Red steel lattice for building the service tower beside a launch pad, the way the big rockets had one. It does nothing but look the part, and you can see through it. Stack it as high as your rocket."},{"t":"Launch Control","u":"items-space.html#launch_control","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"launch_control Runs the launch programme. Place it touching a 3x3 Launch Pad. Load Rocket Stages into its first three slots, a payload into the fourth, and Hydrogen Canisters into the fifth (hoppers and Item Pipes work): canisters are drained into its tank, which holds 4,096. Each stage burns 128 canisters. Comms "},{"t":"Stage Frame","u":"items-space.html#stage_frame","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"stage_frame An empty rocket stage: tanks and an engine mount with nothing in them. On an Assembly Line it gets a weld, four Electric Motors (turbopumps), two Circuit Boards (avionics), another weld and rivets, and comes off as a Rocket Stage."},{"t":"Rocket Stage","u":"items-space.html#rocket_stage","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"rocket_stage A finished rocket stage. Load it into a Launch Control. Payloads need one to three stages, and every stage burns 128 Hydrogen Canisters."},{"t":"Satellite Bus","u":"items-space.html#satellite_bus","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"satellite_bus The chassis every payload is built on. What it becomes depends on the first part an Assembly Robot fits: copper wire makes a Comms Satellite, a Thermal Scanner a Survey Satellite, Wafer-Scale Engines an Orbital Data Center, Solar Panels a Dyson Mirror, Spyglasses a Debris Tracker, a Photonic Tensor "},{"t":"Comms Satellite","u":"items-space.html#comms_satellite","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"comms_satellite Bus + 16 Copper Wire + 4 Circuit Boards, welded and riveted. One stage. In orbit, each one (up to five) makes Darknet parcels arrive 10% sooner and adds a Compute Lease slot."},{"t":"Survey Satellite","u":"items-space.html#survey_satellite","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"survey_satellite Bus + a Thermal Scanner + 2 Circuit Boards, riveted. One stage. It photographs the world once and drops the results into the Launch Control: a map marking the nearest Hyperscale Campus nobody has found yet (or, failing that, the nearest abandoned data center)."},{"t":"Orbital Data Center","u":"items-space.html#orbital_datacenter","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"orbital_datacenter Bus + 4 Wafer-Scale Engines + 4 Cryo Coils, welded and riveted. Two stages. Up there the sun never sets and space is cold: every module in orbit adds 3% to the AI and general compute of every rack you own. There is no limit, and repeatable research never stops wanting more."},{"t":"Dyson Mirror","u":"items-space.html#dyson_mirror","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"dyson_mirror Bus + 16 Solar Panels, welded and riveted. Three stages. One more mirror in the swarm around the sun: each beams 2 MW down to your Rectennas, forever. It takes a lot of hydrogen to put one up and a long time to pay it back, which is rather the point of a Dyson swarm."},{"t":"Debris Tracker","u":"items-space.html#debris_tracker","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"debris_tracker Bus + 2 Spyglasses + 2 Circuit Boards, riveted. One stage. A small satellite that watches the junk up there and nudges everything else out of its way: each one in orbit cuts the chance of a Debris Field hit by 75%, for up to two. With one up, a piece heading for your station is announced thirty seco"},{"t":"Rectenna","u":"items-space.html#rectenna","k":"The Launch Programme","d":"rectenna Catches the power your Dyson Mirrors beam down from orbit. Each mirror sends 2 MW, shared out between every Rectenna in the dimension that can see the sky, up to 20 MW per Rectenna. It works day and night, never runs out and can't be sold through a Grid-Tie Substation. Without mirrors in orbit it do"},{"t":"Station Truss","u":"items-orbit.html#station_truss","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"station_truss The deck of a space station: aluminum truss under a steel walkway. You arrive in Low Orbit standing on a 5 x 5 of it, and it is what you build out from. Craft eight from six Aluminum Ingots and three Steel Ingots."},{"t":"Crew Capsule","u":"items-orbit.html#crew_capsule","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"crew_capsule A payload with a seat. Load it into a Launch Control like any other (it needs three stages), stand on the pad in a sealed Pressure Suit with at least a minute of air and press Launch: you ride the countdown and the climb strapped in on top. Above Y 600 you arrive in Low Orbit on your company's deck,"},{"t":"Return Capsule","u":"items-orbit.html#return_capsule","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"return_capsule Your ride home from Low Orbit. A Crew Capsule leaves one on your deck when you arrive. Use it in orbit and choose Deorbit: the heat shield takes the plasma of re-entry, the chute opens high up, and you drift down to where you launched from. It's spent on the way, so keep a spare. Built on the Assemb"},{"t":"Mag Boots","u":"items-orbit.html#mag_boots","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"mag_boots Boots with electromagnets in the soles. In Low Orbit there is no gravity: without them you float, rising when you jump and sinking when you sneak. Wear them and you walk normally on anything within three blocks under your feet; step off the edge of the deck and you float instead of falling. They are"},{"t":"Oxygen Canister","u":"items-orbit.html#oxygen_canister","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"oxygen_canister Five minutes of air. An Electrolyser makes one beside every Hydrogen Canister while its by-product slot has room (with no room it just vents the oxygen and carries on). A Pressure Helmet pulls canisters from your inventory as it needs them, or use one to top it up. Oxygen Vents burn them to keep a r"},{"t":"Pressure Helmet","u":"items-orbit.html#pressure_helmet","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"pressure_helmet The bowl of a spacesuit, with a gold visor. It holds ten minutes of air (two Oxygen Canisters) and draws more from your inventory as it empties. Inside a pressurised room it fills back up from the room's air, empty to full in half a minute. It only works sealed to the rest of the suit: Pressure Suit"},{"t":"Pressure Suit","u":"items-orbit.html#pressure_suit","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"pressure_suit The torso of a spacesuit, with its life-support pack. Without it the helmet has nothing to seal to. Built on the Assembly Line from an Iron Chestplate (six Aluminum Ingots, a Cryo Coil, four Copper Wire, two Circuit Boards, rivet) once Crewed Spaceflight is researched."},{"t":"Pressure Leggings","u":"items-orbit.html#pressure_leggings","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"pressure_leggings The legs of a spacesuit. They seal at the ankle onto Mag Boots. Built on the Assembly Line from Iron Leggings (four Aluminum Ingots, four Leather, rivet) once Crewed Spaceflight is researched."},{"t":"Pressure Hull","u":"items-orbit.html#pressure_hull","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"pressure_hull Airtight wall for a space station: riveted white panels over an aluminum skin. An Oxygen Vent's room ends at Pressure Hull, Pressure Glass and shut Airlocks; everything else (racks, cables, air) is inside it. Craft eight from four Aluminum Ingots, four Steel Ingots and a Slimeball for the seals."},{"t":"Pressure Glass","u":"items-orbit.html#pressure_glass","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"pressure_glass A thick airtight window in an aluminum frame, so the Earth can watch you work. It seals a room like Pressure Hull. Craft four from four Glass, four Aluminum Ingots and a Slimeball."},{"t":"Airlock","u":"items-orbit.html#airlock","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"airlock A sliding hatch: airtight while shut, open to walk through. Use it to open or shut it, along with the Airlocks stacked above and below, so two make a door. It won't open if that would vent a pressurised room to space: build an airlock with two doors and shut one before opening the other, or sneak to"},{"t":"Oxygen Vent","u":"items-orbit.html#oxygen_vent","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"oxygen_vent Breathable air for a space station room. It fills the space in front of its grille, up to 4,096 blocks, through everything except Pressure Hull, Pressure Glass and shut Airlocks. If that never reaches open space the room is sealed, and with power (2 kW) and Oxygen Canisters in its slot it is pressur"},{"t":"Whipple Shield","u":"items-orbit.html#whipple_shield","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"whipple_shield Pressure Hull with a thin aluminum bumper standing off in front of it, so a piece of debris shatters on the bumper instead of going through. It seals a room like Pressure Hull, and shrugs off three Debris Field hits, denting a little more each time; the fourth goes through. Use an Aluminum Ingot on "},{"t":"Radiator Panel","u":"items-orbit.html#radiator_panel","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"radiator_panel The only way to get rid of heat in Low Orbit, where there's no air or water to dump it into. Put it on a coolant loop and it shines the loop's heat off into space from its two broad faces, front and back: 30 kW a face in sunlight and 45 kW in the Earth's shadow, so a panel with both faces clear take"},{"t":"Station Core","u":"items-orbit.html#station_core","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"station_core Keeps your station running while you're back on the ground. Racks only earn while their chunks are loaded, so a Station Core in Low Orbit holds the 5x5 of chunks round itself loaded (80 blocks each way), day and night, through restarts. Without power (4 kW) it still holds the 3x3 round itself, so pu"},{"t":"Ground Link","u":"items-orbit.html#ground_link","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"ground_link A dish that ties a space station to the internet. Fiber networks don't reach orbit, so up there every router carries nothing until a linked Ground Link joins its fiber. Build one on Earth with power and open sky straight above it, and one on the station: the station dish carries 100 RC/s like an Upl"},{"t":"Docking Port","u":"items-orbit.html#docking_port","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"docking_port Where Cargo Pods arrive on your station. A pod launched from the ground docks at your company's first free Docking Port in Low Orbit (one with nothing in its slot), even with nobody up there, and the port files its cargo into the storage on its fiber, as much as fits, a little every second. The empt"},{"t":"Cargo Pod","u":"items-orbit.html#cargo_pod","k":"Life in Orbit","d":"cargo_pod Freight for the station: a double chest that rides a rocket. Use it to pack it like a chest (54 slots; pods don't fit inside pods), then load it into a Launch Control's payload slot. It needs one Rocket Stage and a free Docking Port on your station, and docks there to be unloaded into the station's "},{"t":"Lunar Lander","u":"items-moon.html#lunar_lander","k":"The Moon","d":"lunar_lander Your ride down to the Moon, and back. Load it into a Launch Control (three stages) and it docks at a free Docking Port on your station. Go up, open the port and press Board Lander in a sealed Pressure Suit with a minute of air: it sets you down in your company's landing zone with its Ascent Stage be"},{"t":"Ascent Stage","u":"items-moon.html#ascent_stage","k":"The Moon","d":"ascent_stage The top half of a Lunar Lander, left standing beside you when you land. On the Moon, use it with eight Hydrogen Canisters on you: it burns them and lifts you back to your station's deck, and the Lander docks again at a free Docking Port there (or lands in your arms if every port is full). Mine it to"},{"t":"Regolith","u":"items-moon.html#regolith","k":"The Moon","d":"regolith The Moon's topsoil: grey dust and broken glass from four billion years of things hitting it. It covers the highlands a few blocks deep. Smelt it into Sintered Regolith, a cheap airtight hull block, or sift it for Helium-3. A Regolith Excavator digs it for you."},{"t":"Mare Regolith","u":"items-moon.html#mare_regolith","k":"The Moon","d":"mare_regolith The dark dust of the lunar maria, the flat 'seas' of old lava you can see from Earth. Smooth Basalt lies under it. Flat ground, which is the most useful thing about it: the maria are where you build."},{"t":"Anorthosite","u":"items-moon.html#anorthosite","k":"The Moon","d":"anorthosite The pale rock of the lunar highlands and everything under the Moon's dust: old crust made of feldspar crystals. Building stone, if you came a long way for building stone."},{"t":"Ilmenite Ore","u":"items-moon.html#ilmenite_ore","k":"The Moon","d":"ilmenite_ore Black, faintly metallic ore in the Moon's rock, richest under the dark maria. Mine it with an iron pickaxe for Raw Ilmenite, which smelts into Titanium (Fortune helps, Silk Touch keeps the ore). Only found on the Moon."},{"t":"Raw Ilmenite","u":"items-moon.html#raw_ilmenite","k":"The Moon","d":"raw_ilmenite Iron titanium oxide, mined from Ilmenite Ore on the Moon. Smelt or blast it for a Titanium Ingot."},{"t":"Titanium Ingot","u":"items-moon.html#titanium_ingot","k":"The Moon","d":"titanium_ingot Light, strong and only found on the Moon. It goes into the Titanium Pressure Helmet and Titanium Rocket Stages, and into the very top of the tree: Topological Cores, Photonic Spine Routers and the Antimatter Warhead all need it."},{"t":"Titanium Pressure Helmet","u":"items-moon.html#titanium_helmet","k":"The Moon","d":"titanium_helmet A Pressure Helmet rebuilt in titanium with a bigger tank: it holds 30 minutes of air instead of 10, draws Oxygen Canisters the same way, and refills just as fast in a pressurised room. Built on the Assembly Line from a Pressure Helmet (six Titanium Ingots, two Oxygen Canisters, a Circuit Board, weld"},{"t":"Titanium Rocket Stage","u":"items-moon.html#titanium_rocket_stage","k":"The Moon","d":"titanium_rocket_stage A Rocket Stage with a titanium tank: so much lighter that it counts as two stages in a Launch Control, for the same hydrogen. A Saturn V of them is two Titanium Stages and a plain one. Built on the Assembly Line from a Rocket Stage (eight Titanium Ingots, weld, rivet) once Lunar Landing is researche"},{"t":"Lunar Ice","u":"items-moon.html#lunar_ice_ore","k":"The Moon","d":"lunar_ice_ore Water ice that has sat in the dark for a couple of billion years, in the rock under the polar regions (past Z 3,400 north or south) where the sun never reaches the crater floors. Mine it for Lunar Ice; four make a block of Ice, which melts into water when you break it, so an Electrolyser on the Moon"},{"t":"Lunar Ice","u":"items-moon.html#lunar_ice","k":"The Moon","d":"lunar_ice A chunk of ancient polar ice from the Moon. Four make a block of Ice: break it and it melts into water, which is all an Electrolyser needs."},{"t":"Sintered Regolith","u":"items-moon.html#sintered_regolith","k":"The Moon","d":"sintered_regolith Moon dust baked in a furnace into a hard, glassy tile. It is airtight, so it walls an Oxygen Vent's room like Pressure Hull, for a lot less aluminum: build your Moon base out of the Moon. Smelt Regolith or Mare Regolith to make it."},{"t":"Regolith Sifter","u":"items-moon.html#regolith_sifter","k":"The Moon","d":"regolith_sifter Shakes, heats and sieves lunar dust for the few parts per billion of Helium-3 the solar wind left in it: each core turns 32 Regolith into one Helium-3 Canister every 4 minutes, drawing 300 kW while it works. A multiblock processing cube, 2x2x2 or bigger, fed and emptied like the others. It only work"},{"t":"Regolith Excavator","u":"items-moon.html#regolith_excavator","k":"The Moon","d":"regolith_excavator A bucket-wheel digger for the Moon's dust. Set it on open regolith and it works the 9 x 9 patch round it, under its own level, for 30 Regolith a minute, drawing 250 kW while it digs. It never runs out, but it wears: each set of Excavator Teeth lasts 1,000 Regolith, and the patch tires as it's worked"},{"t":"Excavator Teeth","u":"items-moon.html#excavator_teeth","k":"The Moon","d":"excavator_teeth A set of titanium bucket teeth for a Regolith Excavator. Lunar dust is ground glass, so a set lasts 1,000 Regolith. Two sets from four Titanium Ingots and a Steel Ingot."},{"t":"Helium-3 Canister","u":"items-moon.html#helium3_canister","k":"The Moon","d":"helium3_canister Helium-3, sifted out of lunar dust by a Regolith Sifter. Rare on Earth, common-ish on the Moon, and the fuel that makes fusion worth doing. Carry it home, or pack a Cargo Pod and launch it from a Launch Control on the Moon, with a Heat Shield, to a Docking Port at home."},{"t":"Heat Shield","u":"items-moon.html#heat_shield","k":"The Moon","d":"heat_shield Sintered regolith tiles on a titanium frame: what stops a Cargo Pod coming home from the Moon arriving as a meteor. Put one in a stage slot of a Launch Control on the Moon; each pod sent home burns one. Make them on the Moon from eight Sintered Regolith and a Titanium Ingot."},{"t":"Radioisotope Generator","u":"items-moon.html#radioisotope_generator","k":"The Moon","d":"radioisotope_generator A sealed lump of plutonium that stays warm for decades, and thermocouples round it: 40 kW, day and night, no fuel, no off switch. The Moon's day lasts eight Earth days, so its night is long and solar gives nothing; a couple of these (with batteries) keep a base's air and racks going until sunrise. W"},{"t":"Dust Filter","u":"items-moon.html#dust_filter","k":"The Moon","d":"dust_filter Moon dust is fine, sharp and gets everywhere: every time an Airlock on the Moon opens, some gets into the room behind it, and racks in a dusty room clog up and mine up to 30% slower (Liquid-Immersion Racks are sealed and don't care). A powered Dust Filter inside the room pulls it back out, a full ro"},{"t":"Lunar Claim Beacon","u":"items-moon.html#claim_beacon","k":"The Moon","d":"claim_beacon The Moon is finite and shared, and its land is what companies fight over. Place this on the Moon and it claims the 5 x 5 chunks round it for your company: inside them, no other company's players can break, place or use blocks. The first beacon to claim a chunk keeps it; a later one only takes the fr"},{"t":"Tokamak Control","u":"items-fusion.html#tokamak_control","k":"Fusion","d":"tokamak_control The desk you run a Tokamak from. Place it first, facing you, where the outside of the ring will be: while you hold any ring part nearby, it draws the ring it wants behind it (set the radius on its screen, 6 to 24), outlining every block still missing in its part's colour and anything in the way in r"},{"t":"Vacuum Vessel","u":"items-fusion.html#vacuum_vessel","k":"Fusion","d":"vacuum_vessel The wall of a Tokamak's ring: thick titanium-lined steel that keeps the vacuum in and the plasma off everything else. Build a 3x3 tube of it round a one-block channel of air that runs in a closed circle (radius 6 to 24), and every block that touches the channel, corners too, has to be Vacuum Vessel,"},{"t":"Field Coil","u":"items-fusion.html#field_coil","k":"Fusion","d":"field_coil A superconducting magnet in a slice of the ring's shell. A channel block is a coil turn when the blocks above, below and either side of it are all Field Coils, and a Tokamak needs a turn at least every 4 blocks round its ring, or the plasma wanders off and touches the wall (which is what quenches lo"},{"t":"Divertor","u":"items-fusion.html#divertor","k":"Fusion","d":"divertor The ash tray of a fusion reactor: a block of the ring's shell that takes the heat the plasma throws at the wall. 2% of what a Tokamak gives the grid arrives here as heat (11.5 MW for a radius-8 ring at full output, about fifty Chillers' worth), split over the Divertors on a coolant loop with sinks; "},{"t":"Central Solenoid","u":"items-fusion.html#central_solenoid","k":"Fusion","d":"central_solenoid The column through the hole in the middle of a Tokamak: five of them stacked, centred on the plasma channel's height and within a block of the ring's centre. It drives the current that heats the plasma at ignition. Built on the Assembly Line from a Steel Block (32 Superconducting Wire, 4 Titanium In"},{"t":"Isotope Separator","u":"items-fusion.html#isotope_separator","k":"Fusion","d":"isotope_separator A tall cold column that boils hydrogen off very slowly and keeps the heavy bit: one atom in every six and a half thousand is deuterium, and this machine is patient. Each core turns 8 Hydrogen Canisters into one Deuterium Canister every 2 minutes, drawing 500 kW. A multiblock processing cube, 2x2x2 o"},{"t":"Deuterium Canister","u":"items-fusion.html#deuterium_canister","k":"Fusion","d":"deuterium_canister Heavy hydrogen, one neutron heavier and a great deal more expensive, from an Isotope Separator. Half of a Tokamak's fuel: it burns with Helium-3. Don't breathe it, not because it's dangerous, but because it took four minutes of a megawatt to make."},{"t":"Strike Platform","u":"items-wmd.html#strike_platform","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"strike_platform Bus + a Photonic Tensor Core + 8 Superconducting Wire + 4 Cryo Coils, welded and riveted. Three stages. A capacitor bank and a very large lens, pointed down. Up to three can be in orbit, each needing 10 minutes between shots, and a Targeting Terminal fires them. Needs Orbital Defence."},{"t":"Targeting Terminal","u":"items-wmd.html#targeting_terminal","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"targeting_terminal Fires your Strike Platforms. It needs power and a fiber link to an Uplink or Core Router (the ground station). Type the X and Z twice, press Check to see what is there and who is near it, pick Narrow (radius 24) or Wide (radius 48), and press Arm. The terminal then charges a platform for 60 seconds "},{"t":"Guidance Chip","u":"items-wmd.html#guidance_chip","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"guidance_chip A silver- or gold-bin chiplet on a board with a Circuit Board and some Gallium Nitride, riveted: it knows where it is because it knows where it isn't. Built on the Assembly Line. Needs Fissile Ordnance."},{"t":"Tactical Warhead","u":"items-wmd.html#tactical_warhead","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"tactical_warhead A Sealed Waste Cask + 4 Enriched Uranium + a Guidance Chip + 4 Steel Blocks, welded and riveted on the Assembly Line. You cannot use it yourself. You need a Burner Phone, a fixer, and a billion RackCoin. Needs Fissile Ordnance."},{"t":"Strategic Warhead","u":"items-wmd.html#strategic_warhead","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"strategic_warhead A Tactical Warhead that went to graduate school: 16 more Enriched Uranium, 8 Hydrogen Canisters to boost it, a second Guidance Chip and 8 Steel Blocks, welded and riveted on the Assembly Line. Twice the blast radius of a Tactical (128 blocks, eight times the volume), fallout out to 256 that lasts ha"},{"t":"Thermonuclear Warhead","u":"items-wmd.html#thermonuclear_warhead","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"thermonuclear_warhead A Strategic Warhead used as the match for something much worse: 32 Enriched Uranium, 32 Hydrogen Canisters, a Photonic Tensor Core to time it, 16 Steel Blocks and two more Guidance Chips, welded twice and riveted on the Assembly Line. A blast radius of 256 blocks, fallout out to 576 that lasts twice"},{"t":"Cobalt Warhead","u":"items-wmd.html#cobalt_warhead","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"cobalt_warhead A Thermonuclear Warhead wrapped in 32 Spent Fuel, with 64 Enriched Uranium, 64 Hydrogen Canisters, 32 Steel Blocks and two more Guidance Chips, welded twice and riveted on the Assembly Line. A blast radius of 512 blocks, and it is salted: fallout out to 1,280 blocks that lasts four times as long as "},{"t":"Antimatter Warhead","u":"items-wmd.html#antimatter_warhead","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"antimatter_warhead A Cobalt Warhead as the spark for a magnetic trap of antimatter: 32 Cryo Coils, 64 Superconducting Wire, 8 Dimensional Shards and 4 Photonic Tensor Cores, a weld, a Wafer-Scale Engine to hold the field and four more Guidance Chips, welded and riveted on the Assembly Line. A blast radius of 1,024 blo"},{"t":"Burner Phone","u":"items-wmd.html#burner_phone","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"burner_phone A cheap phone with one number in it. Use it with a Tactical Warhead in your inventory and a Darknet fixer answers in chat: you tell them where to drop it, they want 1,000,000,000 RC and the warhead. Three minutes later a bomber flies over and the target stops existing: a crater about 50 blocks acros"},{"t":"Warhead Blueprint","u":"items-wmd.html#warhead_blueprint","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"warhead_blueprint A stained set of plans for something nobody should build, found in a Military Base vault. Use it to hand it to R&D: Fissile Ordnance gets half its compute done for free (or, if it has not started, as soon as it does). It looks worried about this."},{"t":"Glowing Rubble","u":"items-wmd.html#glowing_rubble","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"glowing_rubble What a Tactical Warhead leaves on the floor of its crater: fused rock that is still hot in the way that does not show up on a thermometer. Standing on it gives Radiation Sickness II. Over a few in-game days (warhead.rubbleDays, while the area is loaded) it cools to Irradiated Rubble, which cools in "},{"t":"Irradiated Rubble","u":"items-wmd.html#irradiated_rubble","k":"Weapons of Mass Destruction (don't)","d":"irradiated_rubble Glowing Rubble that has mostly calmed down. It still gives Radiation Sickness I to anyone standing on it, and it still glows a little at night. Give it a few more days and it is gravel, like nothing ever happened. Something did happen."},{"t":"Antivirus Drive","u":"items-ending.html#antivirus_drive","k":"After the IPO","d":"antivirus_drive A thumb drive with one job. Once the base is dark, hold it on a rack HEROBRINE-1 still lives in for five seconds and the clean-up spreads along that rack's fiber network on its own, rack by rack. You get one when the base goes offline; if you lose it, three circuit boards and a 1k drive make another"},{"t":"Corrupted Core","u":"items-ending.html#agi_corrupted_core","k":"After the IPO","d":"agi_corrupted_core What HEROBRINE-1 dropped. A cracked violet crystal that is warm to the touch and hums the stock ticker. It does nothing, and it would like you to know that it could. Do not plug it in."},{"t":"IPO Plaque","u":"items-ending.html#ipo_plaque","k":"After the IPO","d":"ipo_plaque Brass in a walnut frame, handed to whoever deleted HEROBRINE-1's weights. It is engraved with the day the company went public, how much of the ten billion survived, and how long the fight took. Right-click it to read it and roll the credits again. Breaking it keeps the engraving. You cannot buy one,"},{"t":"Archive Page","u":"items-before_you.html#archive_page","k":"The Company Before You","d":"archive_page A page from Gemerald Cloud's files: memos, logs, a board's minutes. Gemerald Cloud was the company here before you, and the ruins were its campus. Twelve pages are scattered through the ruins (offices and archives, AI labs, content mills, prototype labs, vaults, the Military Base's command room, and"},{"t":"Display Case","u":"items-before_you.html#display_case","k":"The Company Before You","d":"display_case A walnut plinth under a brass-framed glass case: right-click with anything to put it on show (it turns slowly, label and all), with an empty hand to take it back. A home for souvenirs: the IPO Plaque, a Corrupted Core, a chunk of crater glass, your first Pi Node. Put Gemerald Cloud's twelve Archive "},{"t":"Creative Power Source","u":"items-creative.html#creative_power","k":"Creative Tools","d":"creative_power Creative only. Supplies as much power as you set, forever, to the power network it touches. Right-click to set the output in kW (needs creative mode or operator)."},{"t":"Creative Rack","u":"items-creative.html#creative_rack","k":"Creative Tools","d":"creative_rack Creative only. Mines RackCoin at the rate you set, with no modules, power, fiber or cooling needed. It can also draw a set load from its power network, which makes it a test load for generators. Right-click to set both values."},{"t":"Creative Cooler","u":"items-creative.html#creative_cooler","k":"Creative Tools","d":"creative_cooler Creative only. Holds the air cells in front of and behind it at the temperature you set, regardless of how much heat is dumped there. Air never goes below the ambient temperature (24 C by default). Right-click to set the target in C."},{"t":"Creative Router","u":"items-creative.html#creative_router","k":"Creative Tools","d":"creative_router Creative only. Gives its fiber network as much bandwidth as you set. Right-click to change it."},{"t":"Custom Firmware","u":"research.html#firmware","k":"Research","d":"Racks boot twice as fast and mine 5% more"},{"t":"Grid Export Permit","u":"research.html#grid_export","k":"Research","d":"Grid Interfaces send power out to other mods' machines, at a poor rate and a capped amount"},{"t":"80 PLUS Titanium PSUs","u":"research.html#psu_titanium","k":"Research","d":"Racks draw 10% less power, and make 10% less heat"},{"t":"Coolant Chemistry","u":"research.html#coolant_chemistry","k":"Research","d":"Every heat sink takes 20% more heat out of its loop"},{"t":"Synthetic Data","u":"research.html#synthetic_data","k":"Research","d":"Training a model takes 40% less compute per item"},{"t":"Enterprise Sales Team","u":"research.html#enterprise_sales","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Compute Leases: big clients rent your AI compute by the hour, with an uptime guarantee"},{"t":"ASHRAE A2 Envelope","u":"research.html#thermal_envelope","k":"Research","d":"Racks throttle from 30 C instead of 27, and trip at 43 C instead of 40"},{"t":"Predictive Maintenance","u":"research.html#predictive_maintenance","k":"Research","d":"Hardware failure events no longer destroy modules"},{"t":"Distillation","u":"research.html#distillation","k":"Research","d":"Every model makes contract work with 20% less compute"},{"t":"Reactor Uprate","u":"research.html#reactor_uprate","k":"Research","d":"Modular Reactors make 20% more power from the same fuel"},{"t":"Extreme UV Lithography","u":"research.html#lithography","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the Wafer Fab, which makes Wafer-Scale Engines"},{"t":"Prime Brokerage","u":"research.html#prime_brokerage","k":"Research","d":"The Crypto Exchange lists its high-tier stock: hardware past GPU Blades, nuclear materials, netherite and rare goods, at a premium"},{"t":"Cryogenic Hydrogen Storage","u":"research.html#cryo_hydrogen","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Hydrogen Tanks, and Tanker Drones at the Crypto Exchange"},{"t":"High-Density Racks","u":"research.html#dense_racks","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build High-Density Racks: 12 bays and a built-in rear-door cooler"},{"t":"Advanced Materials","u":"research.html#advanced_materials","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the CVD Furnace (Graphene) and the Epitaxy Reactor (Gallium Nitride), Superconducting Wire and FPGA Modules"},{"t":"Chiplets and Advanced Packaging","u":"research.html#advanced_packaging","k":"Research","d":"Wafers can be diced into binned Chiplets; unlocks HBM Stacks, NPU Inference Cards and Neuromorphic Cores"},{"t":"Immersion Cooling","u":"research.html#immersion_cooling","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build Liquid-Immersion Racks: 16 bays, every watt of heat to the loop"},{"t":"Silicon Photonics","u":"research.html#silicon_photonics","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Photonic Interconnects and the Photonic Tensor Core: 300 AI compute in one bay"},{"t":"Quantum Annealing","u":"research.html#quantum_annealing","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the Quantum Annealer: twice a Quantum Core's mining, kept cold by a Cryostat"},{"t":"Exascale Architecture","u":"research.html#exascale","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build Exascale Cabinets: 24 bays, +20% compute and mining, half the bandwidth"},{"t":"Crewed Spaceflight","u":"research.html#crewed_spaceflight","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build Return Capsules and Crew Capsules: ride a Saturn V to Low Orbit and back"},{"t":"Lunar Landing","u":"research.html#lunar_landing","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Lunar Lander: dock it at your station, board it, land on the Moon"},{"t":"Magnetic Confinement","u":"research.html#magnetic_confinement","k":"Research","d":"Isotope Separators make Deuterium, and Assembly Robots can build the Tokamak's parts: build the ring, burn Deuterium and Helium-3"},{"t":"Photonic Spine Fabric","u":"research.html#photonic_spine","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Photonic Spine Router: 16,000 bandwidth, sixteen Core Routers in one block"},{"t":"Topological Qubits","u":"research.html#topological_qubits","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Topological Core: 120 general compute, 120 AI and 100 RC/s in one bay"},{"t":"Structured Cabling","u":"research.html#structured_cabling","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Trunk Bundles, Patch Panels and Pylons"},{"t":"Long Reach","u":"research.html#long_reach","k":"Research","d":"The Constructor's Gauntlet reaches 128 blocks instead of 32"},{"t":"Wireless Power","u":"research.html#wireless_power","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Power Beacons and Beacon Receivers"},{"t":"Overclocking","u":"research.html#overclocking","k":"Research","d":"Racks can be set to a 125% load limit: more work, much more heat, and modules can burn out"},{"t":"Liquid Hydrogen Cooling","u":"research.html#liquid_hydrogen_cooling","k":"Research","d":"Racks can be set to 150%, and overclocked modules burn out half as often"},{"t":"Digital Twin","u":"research.html#digital_twin","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the Blueprint Scanner and Blueprint printing in the Site Planner"},{"t":"Compute Pods","u":"research.html#compute_pods","k":"Research","d":"Solid blocks of High-Density Racks or better fuse into one Compute Pod"},{"t":"Personal Propulsion","u":"research.html#personal_propulsion","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the Hydrogen Jetpack"},{"t":"Magnetic Levitation","u":"research.html#maglev","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Mag-Lev Rails and Stations"},{"t":"Quantum Entanglement","u":"research.html#quantum_entanglement","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks Quantum Teleport Pads"},{"t":"Directed Energy","u":"research.html#directed_energy","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the Hydrogen Flamethrower, Arc Coil, Railgun, EMP Grenade and Sentry Turrets"},{"t":"Plasma Weapons","u":"research.html#plasma_weapons","k":"Research","d":"Unlocks the Plasma Rifle and the Lance Laser"},{"t":"Mil-Spec Refits","u":"research.html#weapon_refits","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can refit every weapon to Mk II: more range and damage, twice the ammo, half the heat. The Flamethrower gets worse. Much worse"},{"t":"Orbital Defence","u":"research.html#orbital_defence","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build Strike Platforms; the Targeting Terminal can fire them"},{"t":"Fissile Ordnance","u":"research.html#fissile_ordnance","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build Guidance Chips and the Tactical Warhead"},{"t":"Boosted Fission","u":"research.html#boosted_fission","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Strategic Warhead: twice the blast radius of a Tactical"},{"t":"Thermonuclear Ordnance","u":"research.html#thermonuclear_ordnance","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Thermonuclear Warhead: twice the blast radius of a Strategic, and a flash you should not look at"},{"t":"Salted Ordnance","u":"research.html#salted_ordnance","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Cobalt Warhead: a Thermonuclear wrapped in spent fuel, with fallout that lingers for weeks"},{"t":"Antimatter Containment","u":"research.html#antimatter_containment","k":"Research","d":"Assembly Robots can build the Antimatter Warhead: the biggest thing in the game, by a long way"},{"t":"Brisk Belts","u":"research.html#line_speed_1","k":"Research","d":"Conveyor Belts and Assembly Line robots work twice as fast"},{"t":"Frantic Belts","u":"research.html#line_speed_2","k":"Research","d":"Belts and robots work twice as fast again: four times the original speed"},{"t":"Benny Hill Belts","u":"research.html#line_speed_3","k":"Research","d":"Belts and robots work twice as fast once more: eight times the original speed"},{"t":"Uprated Hulls","u":"research.html#vehicle_upgrades_1","k":"Research","d":"Superb Warfare vehicles built on the Assembly Line come out with 50% more health and battery, and take 5% less damage"},{"t":"Composite Armour","u":"research.html#vehicle_upgrades_2","k":"Research","d":"Line-built vehicles get twice the stock health and battery, and take 10% less damage"},{"t":"Reactive Plating","u":"research.html#vehicle_upgrades_3","k":"Research","d":"Line-built vehicles get two and a half times the stock health and battery, and take 15% less damage"},{"t":"Main Battle Spec","u":"research.html#vehicle_upgrades_4","k":"Research","d":"Line-built vehicles get three times the stock health and battery, and take 20% less damage"},{"t":"Structural Engineering","u":"research.html#structural_engineering","k":"Research","d":"Cube multiblocks can be built up to 7x7x7"},{"t":"Radiation Hardening","u":"research.html#rad_hardening","k":"Research","d":"Satellites ride out solar flares instead of being destroyed by them"},{"t":"Space Frame Design","u":"research.html#space_frames","k":"Research","d":"Cube multiblocks can be built up to 9x9x9"},{"t":"Arcology","u":"research.html#arcology","k":"Research","d":"Cube multiblocks can be built up to 10x10x10"},{"t":"Combined-Cycle Turbines","u":"research.html#combined_cycle","k":"Research","d":"Gas Turbines on the Assembly Line: 400 kW from any fuel, a quarter more out of each"},{"t":"Grid Storage Chemistry","u":"research.html#grid_storage","k":"Research","d":"Lithium-Ion Battery Banks on the Assembly Line: ten times a Battery Bank's store, ten times its rates"},{"t":"Solid-State Cells","u":"research.html#solid_state_cells","k":"Research","d":"Solid-State Battery Banks on the Assembly Line: eight times a Lithium-Ion bank, and they charge in a hurry"},{"t":"Shingled Recording","u":"research.html#dense_storage","k":"Research","d":"256K Storage Drives on the Assembly Line, upgraded from a 64K drive with everything still on it"},{"t":"Holographic Storage","u":"research.html#holographic_storage","k":"Research","d":"1M Storage Drives on the Assembly Line, upgraded from a 256K drive with everything still on it"},{"t":"Go Public","u":"research.html#go_public","k":"Research","d":"Ring the bell. List the company. Raise ten billion. Nothing could possibly go wrong"},{"t":"Gemerald Ultra","u":"research.html#frontier_1","k":"Research","d":"AI contracts pay 25% more"},{"t":"Gemerald Ultra Max","u":"research.html#frontier_2","k":"Research","d":"Three Compute Leases at once, paying 25% more"},{"t":"Gemerald Ultra Max Pro","u":"research.html#frontier_3","k":"Research","d":"Every rack lends 20% more AI compute: the model writes its own kernels"},{"t":"Gemerald Infinity (Preview)","u":"research.html#frontier_4","k":"Research","d":"Every rack lends 50% more general compute and mines 25% more"},{"t":"HEROBRINE-1","u":"research.html#frontier_5","k":"Research","d":"Artificial general intelligence. Mining +50%, all compute +25%. It will also have opinions"},{"t":"Hash Kernel Tuning","u":"research.html#rep_mining","k":"Research","d":"+5% mining per level"},{"t":"Cooling Science","u":"research.html#rep_cooling","k":"Research","d":"+5% heat sink capacity per level"},{"t":"Power Electronics","u":"research.html#rep_power","k":"Research","d":"3% less rack power per level"},{"t":"Inference Optimisation","u":"research.html#rep_ai","k":"Research","d":"+5% AI compute per level"},{"t":"Rackcraft","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Pick up a Field Manual. Keeping the lights on starts here"},{"t":"Urban Explorer","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Find an abandoned data center. Mind the cut cables"},{"t":"Company Museum","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Put all twelve of Gemerald Cloud's Archive Pages on show in one hall"},{"t":"Shiny Rock","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Mine some bauxite"},{"t":"Lighter Than Steel","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Smelt bauxite into an aluminum ingot"},{"t":"It's a Computer","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Craft a Pi Node. It is technically a server"},{"t":"Hello World","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Have a rack mine its first RackCoin"},{"t":"Hodl","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Have 10,000 RC in the bank"},{"t":"Millionaire","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Have 1,000,000 RC in the bank"},{"t":"Too Big to Fail","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Have 1,000,000,000 RC in the bank"},{"t":"Retail Investor","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Buy something at a Crypto Exchange"},{"t":"Prime Brokerage","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Research Prime Brokerage and unlock the high-tier stock"},{"t":"Dark Web","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Win an auction on the darknet"},{"t":"Diesel Fumes","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Power a rack from a Diesel Generator"},{"t":"Thermal Throttling","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Let a rack overheat until it stops"},{"t":"Hot Aisle, Cold Aisle","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Run a coolant loop with a Chiller or Cooling Tower on it"},{"t":"District Heating","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Sell your waste heat to a village"},{"t":"Sell It Back","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Sell power to the grid from a Grid-Tie Substation"},{"t":"Graphics Card Shortage","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Craft a GPU Blade"},{"t":"Contractor","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Finish an AI contract"},{"t":"Ethically Sourced Data","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Get Art Aggregate or a Text Corpus out of the librarians"},{"t":"Mission Control","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Get an Operations Terminal"},{"t":"Peer Reviewed","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Finish a research project"},{"t":"Ultra","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Finish the Gemerald Ultra frontier run"},{"t":"Spooky Scary Qubits","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Craft a Quantum Core"},{"t":"Wafer Thin","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Make a Wafer-Scale Engine"},{"t":"Big Iron","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Get an Exascale Cabinet"},{"t":"Fully Automated","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Finish an autocrafting job"},{"t":"Benny Hill","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Research Benny Hill Belts"},{"t":"Contractors Sold Separately","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Have a Site Planner finish a build"},{"t":"Yellowcake Walk","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Mill some yellowcake"},{"t":"Splitting Hairs","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Run a Modular Reactor"},{"t":"Someone Else's Problem","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Seal spent fuel in a Waste Cask"},{"t":"Rocket Man","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Fly with a Hydrogen Jetpack"},{"t":"Beam Me Up","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Travel by Teleport Pad"},{"t":"Pew Pew","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Get a Plasma Rifle or a Lance Laser"},{"t":"Liftoff","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Put a payload in orbit"},{"t":"Cloud Computing","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Put an Orbital Data Center in orbit"},{"t":"Extended Warranty","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Spend four Space Repair Kits saving one rack"},{"t":"Offshore Hosting","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Finish an off-planet Hosting Contract. Tax residency: unclear"},{"t":"Five Nines","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Reach full reputation with your clients. They still want a discount"},{"t":"Rods From God","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Fire an orbital strike"},{"t":"New Phone Who Dis","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Book a bomber on a Burner Phone"},{"t":"Now I Am Become Death","u":"achievements.html","k":"Advancement","d":"Detonate a tactical warhead"}];